window.__programmeIndexChunk("passages",{"63530b4bd66b89a9c96119c49d9430ea359af57aeea6c8215fddf6559631a984":{"markdown":"# Countable amplification of an arbitrary faithful normal semifinite weight\n\n*CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.*\n\nLet \\(M\\subseteq B(K)\\) be a nonzero von Neumann algebra acting with identity \\(I_K\\), on an arbitrary Hilbert space. Let \\(\\psi\\) be a faithful normal semifinite weight. We construct its countable diagonal-sum weight on \\(M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N_0))\\), prove its exact finite domains, onto double-index GNS model, full closed involution and adjoint, every spectral domain and its modular action. No faithful-state, separability or countable-decomposability hypothesis is imposed.\n\nIf the unit of \\(M\\) is properly infinite, the proved filling-projection construction gives a normal isomorphism of this amplification back onto \\(M\\). The transported weight has infinite total mass. Every actual given modular period of \\(\\psi\\) is retained. In particular this supplies the finite-to-infinite weight step needed before the conditional generalized-trace construction, without supplying an inner period or a period from a type invariant.\n\nThe exact earlier local inputs are [VD2](OA-FLOW-VD.md#vd-full-tensor) for the complete bounded-array algebra, finite-corner norms and positivity; [GW1–4](OA-FLOW-GW.md#oa-flow.gw.1) for finite ideals, GNS and the actual semifiniteness criterion; [NF5](OA-FLOW-NF.md#oa-flow.nf.5) and [ST2](OA-FLOW-ST12.md#oa-flow.st.2) for faithful normal GNS and its inverse; [WR3](OA-FLOW-WR.md#oa-flow.wr.3), [CI1 and CI3](OA-FLOW-CI.md#oa-flow.ci.1), and [MW4](OA-FLOW-MW.md#oa-flow.mw.4) for the full finite-star involution, its antilinear adjoint/polar data and arbitrary-weight modular implementation; [SF, SB4–6](OA-FLOW-SF.md#oa-flow.sf.sb4) for full spectral domains, direct sums and spectral transport; [CP1–6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.1) for the norm-closed concrete predual and vector-series tests; [CF6–8](OA-FLOW-CF.md#oa-flow.cf.6) and [GNS3](OA-FLOW-GNS.md#gns-local-units) for bounded operator and representation norm facts; [GNS7.1](OA-FLOW-GNS.md#gns-lemma-7-1) for complete Hilbert sums; [PC5, equation PC6](OA-FLOW-PC.md#oa-flow.projection.pc5) for a countable filling family on a properly infinite unit, and [PC8](OA-FLOW-PC.md#oa-flow.projection.pc8) for the type III case; [CT1](OA-FLOW-CT.md#oa-flow.ct.1) for all-weight full-graph transport. All these are written proofs. In particular equation [PC6](OA-FLOW-PC.md#oa-flow.projection.pc5) belongs to section [PC5](OA-FLOW-PC.md#oa-flow.projection.pc5); section [PC6](OA-FLOW-PC.md#oa-flow.projection.pc6) on finite joins is not the filling theorem.\n\nThe free human context actually read is [Connes, Theorem 4.3.2(a), printed pp.220–221](https://numdam.org/article/ASENS_1973_4_6_2_133_0.pdf#page=89), where amplification by a countable type I factor is used in constructing an infinite weight. No tensor-weight theorem, abstract stability theorem or source's period-existence assertion is used below. The required amplification, graphs and normal representation transport are reconstructed explicitly.\n\n<a id=\"ca-0\"><\/a>\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.ca.0\"><\/a>\n## CA-0. Bounded arrays and amplification of a normal isomorphism\n\nIndex the matrix units by \\(I=\\mathbb N_0\\). [VD2](OA-FLOW-VD.md#oa-flow.vd.2)'s integer-indexed model transports to this indexing through the bijection \\(b_0=0,\\ b_{2j-1}=j,\\ b_{2j}=-j\\) for \\(j\\geq1\\), and the resulting basis unitary. Thus\n\n<a id=\"equation-ca1\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n B=M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I)\n   =\\{X\\in B(\\ell^2(I,K)):X_{ij}\\in M\\text{ for every }i,j\\}.\n \\tag{CA1}\n\\]\nFor a finite set \\(F\\subset I\\), let \\(p_F\\) be the projection onto its coordinates. [VD2](OA-FLOW-VD.md#oa-flow.vd.2) proves\n\n<a id=\"equation-ca2\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n p_FXp_F\\longrightarrow X\\text{ strongly-*},\\quad\n \\|X\\|=\\sup_{F\\ \\mathrm{finite}}\\|p_FXp_F\\|,\\quad\n X\\geq0\\ \\Longleftrightarrow\\\n p_FXp_F\\geq0\\text{ for every finite }F.\n \\tag{CA2}\n\\]\nA consistent array with uniformly bounded finite corner norms defines a unique bounded operator by its bounded form on finite-support vectors and Hilbert completion. Consequently ([CA1](OA-FLOW-CA.md#equation-ca1)) always means bounded arrays, not formal matrices.\n\nWe need a general normal representation transport, beyond [VD2](OA-FLOW-VD.md#oa-flow.vd.2)'s specific diagonal representation. Suppose \\(q:M\\to A\\subseteq B(L)\\) is a normal unital star isomorphism with normal inverse. For each finite \\(F\\), entrywise application \\(q_F:M_F(M)\\to M_F(A)\\) is a bijective star homomorphism. Both are concrete C\\*-algebras. The elementary contractivity proof for unital star homomorphisms, applied to \\(q_F\\) and its inverse, makes \\(q_F\\) isometric; it preserves positivity in both directions by applying it to square roots. The criterion ([CA2](OA-FLOW-CA.md#equation-ca2)) therefore gives an isometric bijection of all bounded arrays\n\n<a id=\"equation-ca3\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n q^{(\\infty)}:M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I)\n       \\longrightarrow A\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I),\\qquad\n (q^{(\\infty)}X)_{ij}=q(X_{ij}).\n \\tag{CA3}\n\\]\nIt preserves adjoints and positivity. For products, each entry of \\(XY\\) is the bounded strong limit of \\(\\sum_{k\\in F}X_{ik}Y_{kj}\\): insert \\(p_F\\) between \\(X,Y\\). Its norm is at most \\(\\|X\\|\\|Y\\|\\). CP's bounded tail estimate gives ultraweak convergence. Normality of \\(q\\) then makes its image the corresponding entry of the target product, proving multiplicativity.\n\nHere is full normality on the entire space. For two vectors in \\(\\ell^2(I,L)\\), truncation to finite coordinates changes the associated functional on the target unit ball by at most\n\n<a id=\"equation-ca4\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\|\\xi-p_F\\xi\\|\\,\\|\\eta\\|\n       +\\|\\xi\\|\\,\\|\\eta-p_F\\eta\\|.\n \\tag{CA4}\n\\]\nEach finite-coordinate pullback is a finite sum of normal tests of \\(q(X_{ij})\\); compression \\(X\\mapsto X_{ij}\\) is ultraweakly continuous by the coordinate vector-series test. Isometry in ([CA3](OA-FLOW-CA.md#equation-ca3)) gives the same norm bound ([CA4](OA-FLOW-CA.md#equation-ca4)) after pullback. CP's norm-closed predual proves that the limiting pullback is normal. Finally a defining square-summable vector series has a norm-summable series of these pullbacks, bounded by the sum of products of the vector norms. This proves ultraweak continuity on all arrays. Applying the identical argument to \\(q^{-1}\\) proves normality of the inverse. No assertion that every convergent net is uniformly bounded is required.\n\n<a id=\"ca-1\"><\/a>\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.ca.1\"><\/a>","html":"<h1>Countable amplification of an arbitrary faithful normal semifinite weight<\/h1>\n<p><em>CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\subseteq B(K)\\)\">\\(M\\subseteq B(K)\\)<\/span> be a nonzero von Neumann algebra acting with identity <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I_K\\)\">\\(I_K\\)<\/span>, on an arbitrary Hilbert space. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\)<\/span> be a faithful normal semifinite weight. We construct its countable diagonal-sum weight on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N_0))\\)\">\\(M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2(\\mathbb N_0))\\)<\/span>, prove its exact finite domains, onto double-index GNS model, full closed involution and adjoint, every spectral domain and its modular action. No faithful-state, separability or countable-decomposability hypothesis is imposed.<\/p>\n<p>If the unit of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is properly infinite, the proved filling-projection construction gives a normal isomorphism of this amplification back onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. The transported weight has infinite total mass. Every actual given modular period of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\)<\/span> is retained. In particular this supplies the finite-to-infinite weight step needed before the conditional generalized-trace construction, without supplying an inner period or a period from a type invariant.<\/p>\n<p>The exact earlier local inputs are <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-VD.html#vd-full-tensor\">VD2<\/a> for the complete bounded-array algebra, finite-corner norms and positivity; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GW.html#oa-flow.gw.1\">GW1–4<\/a> for finite ideals, GNS and the actual semifiniteness criterion; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NF.html#oa-flow.nf.5\">NF5<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-ST12.html#oa-flow.st.2\">ST2<\/a> for faithful normal GNS and its inverse; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-WR.html#oa-flow.wr.3\">WR3<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CI.html#oa-flow.ci.1\">CI1 and CI3<\/a>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MW.html#oa-flow.mw.4\">MW4<\/a> for the full finite-star involution, its antilinear adjoint/polar data and arbitrary-weight modular implementation; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SF.html#oa-flow.sf.sb4\">SF, SB4–6<\/a> for full spectral domains, direct sums and spectral transport; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.1\">CP1–6<\/a> for the norm-closed concrete predual and vector-series tests; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CF.html#oa-flow.cf.6\">CF6–8<\/a> and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GNS.html#gns-local-units\">GNS3<\/a> for bounded operator and representation norm facts; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GNS.html#gns-lemma-7-1\">GNS7.1<\/a> for complete Hilbert sums; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#oa-flow.projection.pc5\">PC5, equation PC6<\/a> for a countable filling family on a properly infinite unit, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#oa-flow.projection.pc8\">PC8<\/a> for the type III case; <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CT.html#oa-flow.ct.1\">CT1<\/a> for all-weight full-graph transport. All these are written proofs. In particular equation <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#oa-flow.projection.pc5\">PC6<\/a> belongs to section <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#oa-flow.projection.pc5\">PC5<\/a>; section <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#oa-flow.projection.pc6\">PC6<\/a> on finite joins is not the filling theorem.<\/p>\n<p>The free human context actually read is <a href=\"https://numdam.org/article/ASENS_1973_4_6_2_133_0.pdf#page=89\">Connes, Theorem 4.3.2(a), printed pp.220–221<\/a>, where amplification by a countable type I factor is used in constructing an infinite weight. No tensor-weight theorem, abstract stability theorem or source's period-existence assertion is used below. The required amplification, graphs and normal representation transport are reconstructed explicitly.<\/p>\n<h2>CA-0. Bounded arrays and amplification of a normal isomorphism<\/h2>\n<p>Index the matrix units by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I=\\mathbb N_0\\)\">\\(I=\\mathbb N_0\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-VD.html#oa-flow.vd.2\">VD2<\/a>'s integer-indexed model transports to this indexing through the bijection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b_0=0,\\ b_{2j-1}=j,\\ b_{2j}=-j\\)\">\\(b_0=0,\\ b_{2j-1}=j,\\ b_{2j}=-j\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\geq1\\)\">\\(j\\geq1\\)<\/span>, and the resulting basis unitary. Thus<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n B=M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I)\n   =\\{X\\in B(\\ell^2(I,K)):X_{ij}\\in M\\text{ for every }i,j\\}.\n \\tag{CA1}\n\\]\">\\[\n B=M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I)\n   =\\{X\\in B(\\ell^2(I,K)):X_{ij}\\in M\\text{ for every }i,j\\}.\n \\tag{CA1}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor a finite set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\subset I\\)\">\\(F\\subset I\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_F\\)\">\\(p_F\\)<\/span> be the projection onto its coordinates. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-VD.html#oa-flow.vd.2\">VD2<\/a> proves<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n p_FXp_F\\longrightarrow X\\text{ strongly-*},\\quad\n \\|X\\|=\\sup_{F\\ \\mathrm{finite}}\\|p_FXp_F\\|,\\quad\n X\\geq0\\ \\Longleftrightarrow\\\n p_FXp_F\\geq0\\text{ for every finite }F.\n \\tag{CA2}\n\\]\">\\[\n p_FXp_F\\longrightarrow X\\text{ strongly-*},\\quad\n \\|X\\|=\\sup_{F\\ \\mathrm{finite}}\\|p_FXp_F\\|,\\quad\n X\\geq0\\ \\Longleftrightarrow\\\n p_FXp_F\\geq0\\text{ for every finite }F.\n \\tag{CA2}\n\\]<\/span>\nA consistent array with uniformly bounded finite corner norms defines a unique bounded operator by its bounded form on finite-support vectors and Hilbert completion. Consequently (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CA.html#equation-ca1\">CA1<\/a>) always means bounded arrays, not formal matrices.<\/p>\n<p>We need a general normal representation transport, beyond <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-VD.html#oa-flow.vd.2\">VD2<\/a>'s specific diagonal representation. Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q:M\\to A\\subseteq B(L)\\)\">\\(q:M\\to A\\subseteq B(L)\\)<\/span> is a normal unital star isomorphism with normal inverse. For each finite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>, entrywise application <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_F:M_F(M)\\to M_F(A)\\)\">\\(q_F:M_F(M)\\to M_F(A)\\)<\/span> is a bijective star homomorphism. Both are concrete C*-algebras. The elementary contractivity proof for unital star homomorphisms, applied to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_F\\)\">\\(q_F\\)<\/span> and its inverse, makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_F\\)\">\\(q_F\\)<\/span> isometric; it preserves positivity in both directions by applying it to square roots. The criterion (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CA.html#equation-ca2\">CA2<\/a>) therefore gives an isometric bijection of all bounded arrays<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n q^{(\\infty)}:M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I)\n       \\longrightarrow A\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I),\\qquad\n (q^{(\\infty)}X)_{ij}=q(X_{ij}).\n \\tag{CA3}\n\\]\">\\[\n q^{(\\infty)}:M\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I)\n       \\longrightarrow A\\bar\\otimes B(\\ell^2 I),\\qquad\n (q^{(\\infty)}X)_{ij}=q(X_{ij}).\n \\tag{CA3}\n\\]<\/span>\nIt preserves adjoints and positivity. For products, each entry of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(XY\\)\">\\(XY\\)<\/span> is the bounded strong limit of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_{k\\in F}X_{ik}Y_{kj}\\)\">\\(\\sum_{k\\in F}X_{ik}Y_{kj}\\)<\/span>: insert <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_F\\)\">\\(p_F\\)<\/span> between <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X,Y\\)\">\\(X,Y\\)<\/span>. Its norm is at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|X\\|\\|Y\\|\\)\">\\(\\|X\\|\\|Y\\|\\)<\/span>. CP's bounded tail estimate gives ultraweak convergence. Normality of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> then makes its image the corresponding entry of the target product, proving multiplicativity.<\/p>\n<p>Here is full normality on the entire space. For two vectors in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^2(I,L)\\)\">\\(\\ell^2(I,L)\\)<\/span>, truncation to finite coordinates changes the associated functional on the target unit ball by at most<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|\\xi-p_F\\xi\\|\\,\\|\\eta\\|\n       +\\|\\xi\\|\\,\\|\\eta-p_F\\eta\\|.\n \\tag{CA4}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|\\xi-p_F\\xi\\|\\,\\|\\eta\\|\n       +\\|\\xi\\|\\,\\|\\eta-p_F\\eta\\|.\n \\tag{CA4}\n\\]<\/span>\nEach finite-coordinate pullback is a finite sum of normal tests of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q(X_{ij})\\)\">\\(q(X_{ij})\\)<\/span>; compression <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\mapsto X_{ij}\\)\">\\(X\\mapsto X_{ij}\\)<\/span> is ultraweakly continuous by the coordinate vector-series test. Isometry in (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CA.html#equation-ca3\">CA3<\/a>) gives the same norm bound (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CA.html#equation-ca4\">CA4<\/a>) after pullback. CP's norm-closed predual proves that the limiting pullback is normal. Finally a defining square-summable vector series has a norm-summable series of these pullbacks, bounded by the sum of products of the vector norms. This proves ultraweak continuity on all arrays. Applying the identical argument to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q^{-1}\\)\">\\(q^{-1}\\)<\/span> proves normality of the inverse. No assertion that every convergent net is uniformly bounded is required.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-CA.md","start_offset":0,"end_offset":6393,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"63fd28f78c72c7322072407ca07420c11f1ba2f2d4edfc2b994ed6fd4317f4e9":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.res.imaginary\"><\/a><a id=\"OA-FLOW.RES.IMAGINARY\"><\/a>\n\n## The spectrum lies on the imaginary axis\n\nFor a closed operator, \\(\\lambda\\) belongs to the resolvent set when \\(\\lambda I-\\delta:D(\\delta)\\to X\\) has a bounded everywhere-defined inverse. Lessons 94 and (L2)–(L8) establish precisely that for every \\(\\lambda\\) off the imaginary axis. Thus\n\n<a id=\"equation-l9\"><\/a>\n\n$$\\operatorname{Sp}(\\delta)\\subset i\\mathbb R.\\tag{L9}$$\n\nThe estimates also give \\(\\|(\\lambda I-\\delta)^{-1}\\|\\le C_\\alpha/|\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda|\\) on both half-planes. They do not assert that every point of the imaginary axis is spectral, nor that the generator is bounded or norm densely defined on \\(X\\).\n\n**Problem.** Let \\(\\alpha_tz=e^{i\\omega t}z\\) on \\(\\mathbb C\\). Compute \\(R_\\lambda^+\\) and \\(R_\\lambda^-\\) and compare them with the algebraic inverse.\n\n**Solution.** The generator is multiplication by \\(i\\omega\\). For \\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda>0\\), \\(\\int_0^\\infty e^{-(\\lambda-i\\omega)t}dt=(\\lambda-i\\omega)^{-1}\\). For \\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda<0\\), \\(-\\int_0^\\infty e^{(\\lambda-i\\omega)t}dt=(\\lambda-i\\omega)^{-1}\\) as well. Both agree with \\((\\lambda I-\\delta)^{-1}\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\nThe mathematical source is M. Takesaki, *Theory of Operator Algebras II*, Lemma XI.1.19, printed pages 325–326 ([edition record](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4)). The backward display on page326 needs the minus sign for the convention \\((\\lambda I-\\delta)^{-1}\\); the trivial-action test and the full reversed-action proof (L7)–(L8) establish it. The proof above retains both inverse identities at their exact domains and supplies all integral and earlier generator inputs.","html":"<h2>The spectrum lies on the imaginary axis<\/h2>\n<p>For a closed operator, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span> belongs to the resolvent set when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda I-\\delta:D(\\delta)\\to X\\)\">\\(\\lambda I-\\delta:D(\\delta)\\to X\\)<\/span> has a bounded everywhere-defined inverse. Lessons 94 and (L2)–(L8) establish precisely that for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span> off the imaginary axis. Thus<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\\operatorname{Sp}(\\delta)\\subset i\\mathbb R.\\tag{L9}\\]\">\\[\\operatorname{Sp}(\\delta)\\subset i\\mathbb R.\\tag{L9}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The estimates also give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|(\\lambda I-\\delta)^{-1}\\|\\le C_\\alpha/|\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda|\\)\">\\(\\|(\\lambda I-\\delta)^{-1}\\|\\le C_\\alpha/|\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda|\\)<\/span> on both half-planes. They do not assert that every point of the imaginary axis is spectral, nor that the generator is bounded or norm densely defined on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Problem.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_tz=e^{i\\omega t}z\\)\">\\(\\alpha_tz=e^{i\\omega t}z\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C\\)<\/span>. Compute <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_\\lambda^+\\)\">\\(R_\\lambda^+\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_\\lambda^-\\)\">\\(R_\\lambda^-\\)<\/span> and compare them with the algebraic inverse.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> The generator is multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\omega\\)\">\\(i\\omega\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda&gt;0\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int_0^\\infty e^{-(\\lambda-i\\omega)t}dt=(\\lambda-i\\omega)^{-1}\\)\">\\(\\int_0^\\infty e^{-(\\lambda-i\\omega)t}dt=(\\lambda-i\\omega)^{-1}\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda&lt;0\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda&lt;0\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-\\int_0^\\infty e^{(\\lambda-i\\omega)t}dt=(\\lambda-i\\omega)^{-1}\\)\">\\(-\\int_0^\\infty e^{(\\lambda-i\\omega)t}dt=(\\lambda-i\\omega)^{-1}\\)<\/span> as well. Both agree with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\lambda I-\\delta)^{-1}\\)\">\\((\\lambda I-\\delta)^{-1}\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The mathematical source is M. Takesaki, <em>Theory of Operator Algebras II<\/em>, Lemma XI.1.19, printed pages 325–326 (<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4\">edition record<\/a>). The backward display on page326 needs the minus sign for the convention <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\lambda I-\\delta)^{-1}\\)\">\\((\\lambda I-\\delta)^{-1}\\)<\/span>; the trivial-action test and the full reversed-action proof (L7)–(L8) establish it. The proof above retains both inverse identities at their exact domains and supplies all integral and earlier generator inputs.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L95.md","start_offset":5156,"end_offset":6833,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"63ebfc8f01189ecde38d653e308f1706141508e5c44eb3237e6d4f76b1e12f4c":{"markdown":"## IS2. The whole measure identity and the right-subgroup unitary\n\nThe quotient measure \\(\\mu_\\rho\\) is locally finite and Radon by L43. Its countable compact cover, and the analogous cover of \\(H\\), make both measures sigma-finite. Form their product by HR5, patching finite compact portions; SC's countable monotone convergence makes the result independent of the exhaustion. Define a Borel measure on \\(G\\) by\n\n<a id=\"equation-is15\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\nu(E)=\\int_Y\\int_H1_E(\\sigma(y)h)\\,dh\\,d\\mu_\\rho(y).\n \\tag{IS15}\n\\]\n\nMeasurability follows from (IS14); positive scalar product integration gives countable additivity. For \\(f\\in C_c(G)\\), the inner integral is \\(Qf(y)\\), independently of the chosen representative, so L43 (Q9) gives \\(\\nu(f)=\\int_G f\\rho\\,ds\\). Every compact \\(C\\subseteq G\\) has a nonnegative compact continuous majorant equal to one on \\(C\\), by H0; consequently \\(\\nu(C)<\\infty\\). In fact \\(\\nu\\) is locally finite, since every point has a relatively compact neighborhood.\n\nHere is the regularity step that permits passage from continuous tests to all Borel sets. A finite Borel measure on a metric space is approximable between closed and open sets. For an open \\(O\\ne X\\), the closed sets \\(\\{d(x,X\\setminus O)\\ge1/n\\}\\) increase to \\(O\\); for a closed \\(F\\), its open \\(1/n\\)-neighborhoods decrease to \\(F\\). Finite-measure continuity gives both approximations. The sets admitting, for every \\(\\epsilon>0\\), closed \\(F\\subseteq E\\subseteq O\\) with measure of \\(O\\setminus F\\) below \\(\\epsilon\\) form a sigma-algebra. Complements preserve the condition. For a countable union choose open errors below \\(\\epsilon2^{-j-2}\\), then use a sufficiently large finite initial union and its closed approximations to make the remaining measure below \\(\\epsilon/2\\). Thus all Borel sets have this property. The case \\(O=X\\), and the empty closed set, are immediate.\n\nFor clarity this local argument suffices for \\(\\nu\\) without assuming a complete metric on the quotient. Decompose \\(G\\) into a countable Borel partition \\(E_j\\), with each \\(E_j\\) contained in a relatively compact open set \\(O_j\\). Apply the finite metric-measure argument to the restriction on \\(O_j\\). Intersect its closed inner approximants with a countable compact exhaustion of \\(O_j\\) to make them compact. For any Borel \\(E\\), finite unions of these compact approximants give inner regularity of \\(\\nu(E)\\). To obtain an open outer approximant when \\(\\nu(E)<\\infty\\), approximate each \\(E\\cap E_j\\) by an open set relative to \\(O_j\\), with excess below \\(\\epsilon2^{-j}\\). Their union is open in \\(G\\), contains \\(E\\), and has excess at most \\(\\epsilon\\). Infinite-measure sets need no outer approximation. This proves that \\(\\nu\\) is Radon in the conventions used here.\n\nRadon uniqueness in HR2 now identifies \\(\\nu\\) with \\(\\rho\\,ds\\). Simple functions followed by SC monotone convergence give\n\n<a id=\"equation-is16\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\int_G F(s)\\rho(s)\\,ds\n =\\int_Y\\int_H F(\\sigma(y)h)\\,dh\\,d\\mu_\\rho(y)\n \\qquad(F\\ge0\\text{ Borel}),\n \\tag{IS16}\n\\]\n\nincluding infinite values. The completions also agree under the Borel bijection: a Borel null set on one side has null inverse image, and each completed null subset lies in such a Borel null set. Thus these coordinate changes act on completed \\(L^2\\) spaces and their whole domains.\n\nApply (IS16) to \\(F=|\\xi|^2/\\rho\\). It proves that (IS5) is isometric. Its inverse on all square-integrable classes is\n\n<a id=\"equation-is17\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n (J_\\sigma^*\\eta)(\\sigma(y)h)\n =\\rho(\\sigma(y)h)^{1/2}\\eta(y,h).\n \\tag{IS17}\n\\]\n\nThe Borel inverse (IS14) and the equality of completed null ideals make this well-defined. The expressions are inverses, so \\(J_\\sigma\\) is onto. Simple functions and L24's Hilbert tensor identification give the same unitary after tensoring with any Hilbert space \\(K\\); no separability of that extra factor is used.\n\nFor \\(r\\in H\\), the density law in (IS2) gives\n\n<a id=\"equation-is18\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n (J_\\sigma R_G(r)\\xi)(y,h)\n &=\\Delta_G(r)^{1/2}\\rho(\\sigma(y)h)^{-1/2}\\xi(\\sigma(y)hr)\\\\\n &=\\Delta_G(r)^{1/2}\n \\left(\\frac{\\rho(\\sigma(y)hr)}{\\rho(\\sigma(y)h)}\\right)^{1/2}\n (J_\\sigma\\xi)(y,hr)\\\\\n &=\\Delta_H(r)^{1/2}(J_\\sigma\\xi)(y,hr).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{IS18}\n\\]\n\nIt follows on the entire Hilbert space that\n\n<a id=\"equation-is19\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n J_\\sigma R_G(r)J_\\sigma^*=1_{L^2Y}\\otimes R_H(r),\\qquad\n (R_H(r)\\eta)(h)=\\Delta_H(r)^{1/2}\\eta(hr).\n \\tag{IS19}\n\\]\n\nFor completeness, put \\(\\kappa(g,y)=\\sigma(gy)^{-1}g\\sigma(y)\\in H\\). It is Borel and\n\n<a id=\"equation-is20\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\kappa(g_1g_2,y)=\\kappa(g_1,g_2y)\\kappa(g_2,y),\\qquad\n (J_\\sigma L_gJ_\\sigma^*\\eta)(y,h)\n =\\left(\\frac{\\rho(g^{-1}\\sigma(y))}{\\rho(\\sigma(y))}\\right)^{1/2}\n \\eta(g^{-1}y,\\kappa(g^{-1},y)h).\n \\tag{IS20}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute (IS17); the right-\\(H\\) density factors in numerator and denominator cancel. This is the image of the group generator in (IS4), with the factor reordering of IS6. Its unitarity and full domain follow from \\(J_\\sigma L_gJ_\\sigma^*\\).\n\n<a id=\"is-3\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>IS2. The whole measure identity and the right-subgroup unitary<\/h2>\n<p>The quotient measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu_\\rho\\)\">\\(\\mu_\\rho\\)<\/span> is locally finite and Radon by L43. Its countable compact cover, and the analogous cover of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, make both measures sigma-finite. Form their product by HR5, patching finite compact portions; SC's countable monotone convergence makes the result independent of the exhaustion. Define a Borel measure on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> by<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\nu(E)=\\int_Y\\int_H1_E(\\sigma(y)h)\\,dh\\,d\\mu_\\rho(y).\n \\tag{IS15}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\nu(E)=\\int_Y\\int_H1_E(\\sigma(y)h)\\,dh\\,d\\mu_\\rho(y).\n \\tag{IS15}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Measurability follows from (IS14); positive scalar product integration gives countable additivity. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in C_c(G)\\)\">\\(f\\in C_c(G)\\)<\/span>, the inner integral is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Qf(y)\\)\">\\(Qf(y)\\)<\/span>, independently of the chosen representative, so L43 (Q9) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu(f)=\\int_G f\\rho\\,ds\\)\">\\(\\nu(f)=\\int_G f\\rho\\,ds\\)<\/span>. Every compact <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\subseteq G\\)\">\\(C\\subseteq G\\)<\/span> has a nonnegative compact continuous majorant equal to one on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>, by H0; consequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu(C)&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\nu(C)&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>. In fact <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span> is locally finite, since every point has a relatively compact neighborhood.<\/p>\n<p>Here is the regularity step that permits passage from continuous tests to all Borel sets. A finite Borel measure on a metric space is approximable between closed and open sets. For an open <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O\\ne X\\)\">\\(O\\ne X\\)<\/span>, the closed sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{d(x,X\\setminus O)\\ge1/n\\}\\)\">\\(\\{d(x,X\\setminus O)\\ge1/n\\}\\)<\/span> increase to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O\\)\">\\(O\\)<\/span>; for a closed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>, its open <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/n\\)\">\\(1/n\\)<\/span>-neighborhoods decrease to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>. Finite-measure continuity gives both approximations. The sets admitting, for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\)<\/span>, closed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\subseteq E\\subseteq O\\)\">\\(F\\subseteq E\\subseteq O\\)<\/span> with measure of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O\\setminus F\\)\">\\(O\\setminus F\\)<\/span> below <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\epsilon\\)\">\\(\\epsilon\\)<\/span> form a sigma-algebra. Complements preserve the condition. For a countable union choose open errors below <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\epsilon2^{-j-2}\\)\">\\(\\epsilon2^{-j-2}\\)<\/span>, then use a sufficiently large finite initial union and its closed approximations to make the remaining measure below <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\epsilon/2\\)\">\\(\\epsilon/2\\)<\/span>. Thus all Borel sets have this property. The case <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O=X\\)\">\\(O=X\\)<\/span>, and the empty closed set, are immediate.<\/p>\n<p>For clarity this local argument suffices for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span> without assuming a complete metric on the quotient. Decompose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> into a countable Borel partition <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_j\\)\">\\(E_j\\)<\/span>, with each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_j\\)\">\\(E_j\\)<\/span> contained in a relatively compact open set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O_j\\)\">\\(O_j\\)<\/span>. Apply the finite metric-measure argument to the restriction on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O_j\\)\">\\(O_j\\)<\/span>. Intersect its closed inner approximants with a countable compact exhaustion of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O_j\\)\">\\(O_j\\)<\/span> to make them compact. For any Borel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>, finite unions of these compact approximants give inner regularity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu(E)\\)\">\\(\\nu(E)\\)<\/span>. To obtain an open outer approximant when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu(E)&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\nu(E)&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>, approximate each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\cap E_j\\)\">\\(E\\cap E_j\\)<\/span> by an open set relative to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(O_j\\)\">\\(O_j\\)<\/span>, with excess below <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\epsilon2^{-j}\\)\">\\(\\epsilon2^{-j}\\)<\/span>. Their union is open in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>, contains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>, and has excess at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\epsilon\\)\">\\(\\epsilon\\)<\/span>. Infinite-measure sets need no outer approximation. This proves that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span> is Radon in the conventions used here.<\/p>\n<p>Radon uniqueness in HR2 now identifies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\nu\\)\">\\(\\nu\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\,ds\\)\">\\(\\rho\\,ds\\)<\/span>. Simple functions followed by SC monotone convergence give<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_G F(s)\\rho(s)\\,ds\n =\\int_Y\\int_H F(\\sigma(y)h)\\,dh\\,d\\mu_\\rho(y)\n \\qquad(F\\ge0\\text{ Borel}),\n \\tag{IS16}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_G F(s)\\rho(s)\\,ds\n =\\int_Y\\int_H F(\\sigma(y)h)\\,dh\\,d\\mu_\\rho(y)\n \\qquad(F\\ge0\\text{ Borel}),\n \\tag{IS16}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>including infinite values. The completions also agree under the Borel bijection: a Borel null set on one side has null inverse image, and each completed null subset lies in such a Borel null set. Thus these coordinate changes act on completed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span> spaces and their whole domains.<\/p>\n<p>Apply (IS16) to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F=|\\xi|^2/\\rho\\)\">\\(F=|\\xi|^2/\\rho\\)<\/span>. It proves that (IS5) is isometric. Its inverse on all square-integrable classes is<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (J_\\sigma^*\\eta)(\\sigma(y)h)\n =\\rho(\\sigma(y)h)^{1/2}\\eta(y,h).\n \\tag{IS17}\n\\]\">\\[\n (J_\\sigma^*\\eta)(\\sigma(y)h)\n =\\rho(\\sigma(y)h)^{1/2}\\eta(y,h).\n \\tag{IS17}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The Borel inverse (IS14) and the equality of completed null ideals make this well-defined. The expressions are inverses, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J_\\sigma\\)\">\\(J_\\sigma\\)<\/span> is onto. Simple functions and L24's Hilbert tensor identification give the same unitary after tensoring with any Hilbert space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>; no separability of that extra factor is used.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\in H\\)\">\\(r\\in H\\)<\/span>, the density law in (IS2) gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n (J_\\sigma R_G(r)\\xi)(y,h)\n &amp;=\\Delta_G(r)^{1/2}\\rho(\\sigma(y)h)^{-1/2}\\xi(\\sigma(y)hr)\\\\\n &amp;=\\Delta_G(r)^{1/2}\n \\left(\\frac{\\rho(\\sigma(y)hr)}{\\rho(\\sigma(y)h)}\\right)^{1/2}\n (J_\\sigma\\xi)(y,hr)\\\\\n &amp;=\\Delta_H(r)^{1/2}(J_\\sigma\\xi)(y,hr).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{IS18}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n (J_\\sigma R_G(r)\\xi)(y,h)\n &amp;=\\Delta_G(r)^{1/2}\\rho(\\sigma(y)h)^{-1/2}\\xi(\\sigma(y)hr)\\\\\n &amp;=\\Delta_G(r)^{1/2}\n \\left(\\frac{\\rho(\\sigma(y)hr)}{\\rho(\\sigma(y)h)}\\right)^{1/2}\n (J_\\sigma\\xi)(y,hr)\\\\\n &amp;=\\Delta_H(r)^{1/2}(J_\\sigma\\xi)(y,hr).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{IS18}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>It follows on the entire Hilbert space that<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n J_\\sigma R_G(r)J_\\sigma^*=1_{L^2Y}\\otimes R_H(r),\\qquad\n (R_H(r)\\eta)(h)=\\Delta_H(r)^{1/2}\\eta(hr).\n \\tag{IS19}\n\\]\">\\[\n J_\\sigma R_G(r)J_\\sigma^*=1_{L^2Y}\\otimes R_H(r),\\qquad\n (R_H(r)\\eta)(h)=\\Delta_H(r)^{1/2}\\eta(hr).\n \\tag{IS19}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For completeness, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\kappa(g,y)=\\sigma(gy)^{-1}g\\sigma(y)\\in H\\)\">\\(\\kappa(g,y)=\\sigma(gy)^{-1}g\\sigma(y)\\in H\\)<\/span>. It is Borel and<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\kappa(g_1g_2,y)=\\kappa(g_1,g_2y)\\kappa(g_2,y),\\qquad\n (J_\\sigma L_gJ_\\sigma^*\\eta)(y,h)\n =\\left(\\frac{\\rho(g^{-1}\\sigma(y))}{\\rho(\\sigma(y))}\\right)^{1/2}\n \\eta(g^{-1}y,\\kappa(g^{-1},y)h).\n \\tag{IS20}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\kappa(g_1g_2,y)=\\kappa(g_1,g_2y)\\kappa(g_2,y),\\qquad\n (J_\\sigma L_gJ_\\sigma^*\\eta)(y,h)\n =\\left(\\frac{\\rho(g^{-1}\\sigma(y))}{\\rho(\\sigma(y))}\\right)^{1/2}\n \\eta(g^{-1}y,\\kappa(g^{-1},y)h).\n \\tag{IS20}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Substitute (IS17); the right-<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> density factors in numerator and denominator cancel. This is the image of the group generator in (IS4), with the factor reordering of IS6. Its unitarity and full domain follow from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J_\\sigma L_gJ_\\sigma^*\\)\">\\(J_\\sigma L_gJ_\\sigma^*\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-IS.md","start_offset":9934,"end_offset":14940,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"637c89cbea569c4cce92f36a754d7472aa710fff695a20eafc3f7a587261a4cf":{"markdown":"<a id=\"l128-tc2\"><\/a>\n\n## TC2. This extension is the only trace, up to scale\n\nLet \\(\\rho\\) be another faithful normal semifinite trace on \\(Q\\). Its restriction to \\(eQe\\) is semifinite: any positive finite-weight element below an element supported by \\(e\\) is again supported by \\(e\\). Choose a nonzero projection \\(q\\le e\\) with \\(\\rho(q)<\\infty\\); a nonzero positive finite-weight element and a nonzero positive spectral cutoff produce such a projection. Set \\(a=t(q)>0\\).\n\nThere is a finite orthogonal partition of \\(e\\) into projections each subequivalent to \\(q\\). To construct it, as long as the remaining projection has \\(t\\)-value at least \\(a\\), remove a subprojection equivalent to \\(q\\), using (TC1). After at most \\(\\lfloor1/a\\rfloor\\) removals the remaining value is less than \\(a\\), unless it is zero. The last remainder is subequivalent to \\(q\\) by (TC1). Therefore\n\\[\n 0<\\rho(e)\\le (\\lfloor1/a\\rfloor+1)\\rho(q)<\\infty. \\tag{TC5}\n\\]\nThe normalized restriction of \\(\\rho\\) is a finite normal trace on \\(eQe\\). By TC0 it equals \\(t\\), so \\(\\rho|_{eQe}=c t\\) with \\(c=\\rho(e)>0\\).\n\nFor the same \\(R_F\\) as above, normality and trace symmetry yield\n\\[\n \\rho(x)=\\sup_F\\rho(x^{1/2}R_Fx^{1/2})\n =\\sum_i\\rho(v_i^*xv_i)=cT(x)\\quad(x\\ge0). \\tag{TC6}\n\\]\nThis proves global uniqueness without an imported unbounded Radon–Nikodym theorem or an unproved extension theorem. In particular, on a finite factor every faithful normal semifinite trace is finite, because TC0 already supplies its normalized finite trace.","html":"<h2>TC2. This extension is the only trace, up to scale<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> be another faithful normal semifinite trace on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span>. Its restriction to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(eQe\\)\">\\(eQe\\)<\/span> is semifinite: any positive finite-weight element below an element supported by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span> is again supported by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span>. Choose a nonzero projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\le e\\)\">\\(q\\le e\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(q)&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\rho(q)&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>; a nonzero positive finite-weight element and a nonzero positive spectral cutoff produce such a projection. Set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=t(q)&gt;0\\)\">\\(a=t(q)&gt;0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>There is a finite orthogonal partition of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span> into projections each subequivalent to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span>. To construct it, as long as the remaining projection has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>-value at least <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>, remove a subprojection equivalent to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span>, using (TC1). After at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lfloor1/a\\rfloor\\)\">\\(\\lfloor1/a\\rfloor\\)<\/span> removals the remaining value is less than <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>, unless it is zero. The last remainder is subequivalent to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> by (TC1). Therefore\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n 0&lt;\\rho(e)\\le (\\lfloor1/a\\rfloor+1)\\rho(q)&lt;\\infty. \\tag{TC5}\n\\]\">\\[\n 0&lt;\\rho(e)\\le (\\lfloor1/a\\rfloor+1)\\rho(q)&lt;\\infty. \\tag{TC5}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe normalized restriction of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> is a finite normal trace on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(eQe\\)\">\\(eQe\\)<\/span>. By TC0 it equals <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho|_{eQe}=c t\\)\">\\(\\rho|_{eQe}=c t\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=\\rho(e)&gt;0\\)\">\\(c=\\rho(e)&gt;0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For the same <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_F\\)\">\\(R_F\\)<\/span> as above, normality and trace symmetry yield\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\rho(x)=\\sup_F\\rho(x^{1/2}R_Fx^{1/2})\n =\\sum_i\\rho(v_i^*xv_i)=cT(x)\\quad(x\\ge0). \\tag{TC6}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\rho(x)=\\sup_F\\rho(x^{1/2}R_Fx^{1/2})\n =\\sum_i\\rho(v_i^*xv_i)=cT(x)\\quad(x\\ge0). \\tag{TC6}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis proves global uniqueness without an imported unbounded Radon–Nikodym theorem or an unproved extension theorem. In particular, on a finite factor every faithful normal semifinite trace is finite, because TC0 already supplies its normalized finite trace.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L128.md","start_offset":22630,"end_offset":24146,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"63df717a8c68b2e86e5c25e2a71005903e39e19823395980635cf47b9ead87af":{"markdown":"<a id=\"sec-3\"><\/a>\n## 3. Integrate through the predual, then compare on vectors\n\nThe coefficients of a locally Lusin section need not be measurable for the operator-norm topology of \\(M\\). Their scalar norm and normal functional evaluations are measurable by Section 1. These are the inputs for the operator integral. Genuine Banach-valued integrals will be used after proving strong measurability of the vector functions involved.\n\n**The integral in a fixed fiber.** Fix a degree \\(t\\) and put \\(E=M(t)\\), with the canonical predual \\(E_*\\) constructed in [GRD2](OA-FLOW-GRD.md#grd-2). More generally the following argument applies to any specified dual Banach space \\(E=(E_*)^*\\). Let \\(k:S\\to E\\), where \\(S\\) is \\(\\mathbb R\\) or a finite Cartesian power with completed Lebesgue measure. Suppose that every function \\(s\\mapsto\\ell(k(s))\\), \\(\\ell\\in E_*\\), is measurable, and that\n\\[\n \\|k(s)\\|\\le g(s)\\quad\\text{almost everywhere},\\qquad\n 0\\le g\\in L^1(S).\n \\tag{SEC3.a}\n\\]\nEvery such scalar function is integrable. The rule\n\\[\n \\ell\\longmapsto\\int_S\\ell(k(s))\\,ds\n \\tag{SEC3.b}\n\\]\nis complex linear and bounded on \\(E_*\\), with norm at most \\(\\int_Sg\\). Duality therefore gives a unique element, denoted \\(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\), such that\n\\[\n \\ell\\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)\n   =\\int_S\\ell(k(s))\\,ds\n       \\quad(\\ell\\in E_*).\n \\tag{SEC3.c}\n\\]\nThis is the weak-star integral. In particular, for a locally Lusin fixed-fiber function with integrable norm,\n\\[\n \\left\\|\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right\\|\n     \\le\\int_S\\|k(s)\\|\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC3.d}\n\\]\nFor \\(M\\) the duality used here is proved in [CP6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.6); for \\(M(t)\\) it is transported by the normal isometric coefficient chart. The integration variable \\(s\\) does not change the output degree \\(t\\). No addition of elements from different fibers is involved.\n\nScalar integration and separation by \\(E_*\\) prove linearity, invariance under almost-everywhere changes, and additivity over measurable pieces. They also prove dominated weak-star convergence: if \\(\\ell(k_n(s))\\to\\ell(k(s))\\) almost everywhere for each \\(\\ell\\), with a common integrable bound for \\(\\|k_n(s)\\|\\) and \\(\\|k(s)\\|\\), then the integrals converge weak-star. For this conclusion the scalar null set may depend on the fixed functional being tested.\n\n**Normal linear and conjugate-linear transport.** A weak-star continuous complex-linear functional on \\(E=(E_*)^*\\) belongs to \\(E_*\\): continuity controls it by finitely many predual tests, so it vanishes on their common kernel. It factors through their finite-dimensional image and is consequently a linear combination of those tests. This is the same finite-test argument used in CP6.\n\nLet \\(A:E\\to F\\) be a bounded weak-star continuous complex-linear map between specified dual Banach spaces. Its preadjoint is \\(A_*\\lambda=\\lambda\\circ A\\), so (SEC3.c) gives\n\\[\n A\\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)\n       =\\int_S A(k(s))\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC3.e}\n\\]\nHere the right side exists by the bound \\(\\|A(k(s))\\|\\le\\|A\\|g(s)\\).\n\nThe same identity holds when \\(A\\) is bounded, conjugate linear and weak-star continuous. To prove it, define\n\\[\n (A^\\natural\\lambda)(x)=\\overline{\\lambda(Ax)}\n       \\quad(\\lambda\\in F_*,\\ x\\in E).\n \\tag{SEC3.f}\n\\]\nThis is a complex-linear weak-star continuous functional of \\(x\\), hence belongs to \\(E_*\\). Applying (SEC3.c) to it and then conjugating the scalar integral gives\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\lambda\\!\\left(A\\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)\\right)\n &=\\overline{(A^\\natural\\lambda)\n                    \\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)}\\\\\n &=\\int_S\\lambda(A(k(s)))\\,ds.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC3.g}\n\\]\nAll \\(\\lambda\\in F_*\\) separate the points of \\(F\\), proving (SEC3.e) in this case as well. The map \\(A^\\natural\\) is conjugate linear in \\(\\lambda\\); it is not being called a complex-linear preadjoint.\n\nThese maps also preserve local Mackey–Lusin measurability. Indeed, if \\(K\\subset F_*\\) is absolutely convex and weakly compact, its image under \\(A_*\\), or under \\(A^\\natural\\), is absolutely convex and weakly compact in \\(E_*\\). Weak continuity follows by testing at each fixed \\(x\\); conjugation in (SEC3.f) preserves continuity. The seminorm identity\n\\[\n p_K(Ax)=p_{A_*K}(x)\n \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n p_K(Ax)=p_{A^\\natural K}(x)\n \\tag{SEC3.h}\n\\]\nproves Mackey continuity on the entire space. Compose on each Lusin compact set to get the stated measurability assertion.\n\nThe graded adjoint \\(M(t)\\to M(-t)\\) is a bounded normal conjugate-linear isometry: in a faithful chart it is \\(x\\mapsto\\sigma_{-t}^\\varphi(x^*)\\), by [GRD3](OA-FLOW-GRD.md#grd-3); the adjoint is ultraweakly continuous by CP6's vector-series formula, and the automorphism is normal. Therefore\n\\[\n \\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)^*\n       =\\int_S k(s)^*\\,ds\n       \\quad\\text{in }M(-t).\n \\tag{SEC3.i}\n\\]\nFixed coefficient charts, fixed left or right multipliers, and each fixed modular automorphism satisfy the linear transport formula for the same reason. For example a normal functional evaluated at \\(axb\\) is again a square-summable vector series, with its vectors changed by the fixed bounded operators \\(a,b\\).\n\n**Strong measurability on an arbitrary Hilbert space.** Represent \\(M\\) faithfully and normally on \\(H\\); no separability of \\(H\\) is assumed. Let \\(k:\\mathbb R\\to M\\) be locally Lusin. The countable compact-cover construction of Section 1 supplies compact sets \\(K_j\\) whose union is conull, such that \\(k|_{K_j}\\) is Mackey continuous. The compact-image norm bound of that section and [GRD4–5](OA-FLOW-GRD.md#grd-5) make each restriction strong-star continuous in this representation. Thus, for every fixed \\(\\xi\\in H\\), the two maps\n\\[\n s\\longmapsto k(s)\\xi,\\qquad\n s\\longmapsto k(s)^*\\xi\n \\tag{SEC3.j}\n\\]\nare continuous on each \\(K_j\\).\n\nEach of their compact images in the metric space \\(H\\) is separable: choose a finite \\(1/n\\)-net for every positive integer \\(n\\). The closed linear span of the resulting countable union of sets is separable. Outside the null complement of \\(\\bigcup_jK_j\\), it contains the entire vector range. The maps are norm-Borel measurable after setting them to zero on that complement: the inverse image of an open subset of \\(H\\) is a countable union of relatively open subsets of the compact \\(K_j\\), with the Borel complement added if needed. With the original null values restored they are measurable for completed Lebesgue measure.\n\nThe strong-measurability proof in [L24, Section 4](OA-FLOW-L24.md#oa-flow.grp.vectorintegration) now applies. In a separable essential range, measurable first-nearby-point choices from a countable dense set, followed by finite truncations, give finite-valued measurable approximants almost everywhere. Hence both functions in (SEC3.j) are strongly measurable even when \\(H\\) itself is not separable.\n\nIf \\(\\int\\|k(s)\\|\\,ds<\\infty\\), they have integrable vector norms. L24's Bochner integral is therefore available, and\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\left(\\int k(s)\\,ds\\right)\\xi\n      &=\\int k(s)\\xi\\,ds,\\\\\n \\left(\\int k(s)\\,ds\\right)^*\\xi\n      &=\\int k(s)^*\\xi\\,ds.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC3.k}\n\\]\nFor the first identity, test against each \\(\\eta\\in H\\); the vector functional belongs to \\(M_*\\), so its two scalar integrals agree by (SEC3.c). Hilbert-space separation proves equality. The second follows in the same way, or from (SEC3.i). For a fixed graded fiber first apply its coefficient chart; equivalently use its normal embedding into the core and a faithful normal representation of the core. Thus the comparison respects the canonical fiber integral.\n\nThe argument also handles products with the implementing unitaries needed below. The [faithful-weight implementation theorem](OA-FLOW-MW.md#oa-flow.mw.4) gives a faithful normal GNS representation with\n\\(\\sigma_s^\\varphi(x)=W_sxW_s^*\\), where \\(W_s\\) is strongly continuous. If \\(v(s)\\) is continuous on a compact restriction, then\n\\[\n \\|W_sv(s)-W_{s_0}v(s_0)\\|\n \\le\\|v(s)-v(s_0)\\|+\\|(W_s-W_{s_0})v(s_0)\\|\n \\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SEC3.l}\n\\]\nThe same holds for \\(W_s^*\\). Apply this to (SEC3.j) on its compact restrictions. Their countable compact images again prove strong measurability of fields such as \\(W_s^*k(s)^*\\xi\\). A bound for \\(\\|k(s)\\|\\) supplies the corresponding integrable majorant whenever required.\n\n**Vector translation continuity.** For every strongly measurable \\(F:\\mathbb R\\to H\\) with integrable norm,\n\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\|F(r+h)-F(r)\\|\\,dr\\longrightarrow0\n       \\quad(h\\to0).\n \\tag{SEC3.m}\n\\]\nFor a finite sum \\(S=\\sum_{j=1}^m1_{A_j}\\xi_j\\), with the \\(A_j\\) of finite measure, the left side is at most\n\\(\\sum_j\\|\\xi_j\\|\\,|(A_j-h)\\mathbin{\\triangle}A_j|\\).\nEach term tends to zero by the [scalar translation proof](OA-FLOW-FF.md#oa-flow.ff.2). That proof obtains finite-measure indicator approximation from Lebesgue regularity and bounded intervals. L24 supplies such finite-valued approximants \\(S\\) in integral norm for every \\(F\\). Translation preserves that norm, so\n\\[\n \\|F(\\,\\cdot+h)-F\\|_{L^1(H)}\n \\le2\\|F-S\\|_{L^1(H)}\n       +\\|S(\\,\\cdot+h)-S\\|_{L^1(H)}.\n \\tag{SEC3.n}\n\\]\nFirst choose \\(S\\), then decrease \\(h\\). This proves (SEC3.m).\n\n**The Fubini hypotheses.** We will use vector Fubini only for a strongly measurable function \\(F:\\mathbb R^2\\to H\\) with\n\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\|F(r,s)\\|\\,dr\\,ds<\\infty.\n \\tag{SEC3.o}\n\\]\nL24, Proposition 4.3 proves that its iterated Bochner integrals exist almost everywhere and equal its product integral. Its sigma compact carrier condition holds on \\(\\mathbb R^2\\). The proof approximates in integral norm by finite-valued functions and uses scalar Fubini on a summable series of their norm differences, so it applies to arbitrary \\(H\\).\n\nFor operator identities there is a convenient sufficient hypothesis that also controls exceptional sets. Let \\(K:\\mathbb R^2\\to M(t)\\) be locally Mackey–Lusin on bounded rectangles and suppose that \\(\\|K(r,s)\\|\\le g(r,s)\\) almost everywhere, for a measurable integrable scalar \\(g\\). Countably many compact restrictions cover a conull Borel set, by the same decreasing-loss construction as in Section 1. After setting \\(K=0\\) off this set, each predual pairing is jointly Borel: its restriction to each compact is continuous, and a countable union of the corresponding Borel pieces gives every inverse image. The scalar norm is measurable as well. The [scalar Fubini proof](OA-FLOW-FF.md#scalar-interchange) then gives\n\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R^2}K(r,s)\\,dr\\,ds\n =\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}K(r,s)\\,ds\\right)dr\n =\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}K(r,s)\\,dr\\right)ds,\n \\tag{SEC3.p}\n\\]\nwith all three integrals understood weak-star.\n\nTo justify the operator statement, the set where \\(\\int g(r,s)\\,ds\\) is infinite is one scalar null set, independent of the normal functional. Off it every inner weak-star integral exists by (SEC3.a)–(SEC3.c), since the Borel representative has measurable scalar sections for every functional. Its scalar pairings are measurable functions of \\(r\\), and its norm is bounded by \\(\\int g(r,s)\\,ds\\). The outer weak-star integral exists using that bound, even without a separate assertion of norm measurability of the inner integral. Testing each \\(\\ell\\in M(t)_*\\) and applying scalar Fubini proves the first equality; reverse \\(r,s\\) for the second. If the original field differs on a product-null set, scalar Fubini first removes the null set of rows or columns on which its section need not be null. This single set again does not depend on \\(\\ell\\).\n\nOn each compact restriction, coefficient fields applied to a fixed vector, and their adjoints, are continuous with compact metric image. The argument for (SEC3.j) therefore also proves strong measurability of these joint vector fields. Bound (SEC3.o) follows from \\(g\\|\\xi\\|\\), so (SEC3.p) agrees with the Bochner comparisons whenever they are used.\n\nThese statements iterate to further real variables under an integrable scalar norm majorant. For an additional parameter \\(u\\), if a scalar triple-integral bound makes \\(\\int\\!\\int g_u(r,s)\\,dr\\,ds\\) finite outside a null set of \\(u\\), apply (SEC3.p) for each such \\(u\\) once local Lusin measurability of \\(K_u\\) has been proved. This supplies a common parameter exception chosen from the scalar majorant, rather than from an uncountable family of vector or functional tests.","html":"<h2>3. Integrate through the predual, then compare on vectors<\/h2>\n<p>The coefficients of a locally Lusin section need not be measurable for the operator-norm topology of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. Their scalar norm and normal functional evaluations are measurable by Section 1. These are the inputs for the operator integral. Genuine Banach-valued integrals will be used after proving strong measurability of the vector functions involved.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The integral in a fixed fiber.<\/strong> Fix a degree <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E=M(t)\\)\">\\(E=M(t)\\)<\/span>, with the canonical predual <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_*\\)\">\\(E_*\\)<\/span> constructed in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GRD.html#grd-2\">GRD2<\/a>. More generally the following argument applies to any specified dual Banach space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E=(E_*)^*\\)\">\\(E=(E_*)^*\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k:S\\to E\\)\">\\(k:S\\to E\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R\\)<\/span> or a finite Cartesian power with completed Lebesgue measure. Suppose that every function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\mapsto\\ell(k(s))\\)\">\\(s\\mapsto\\ell(k(s))\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell\\in E_*\\)\">\\(\\ell\\in E_*\\)<\/span>, is measurable, and that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|k(s)\\|\\le g(s)\\quad\\text{almost everywhere},\\qquad\n 0\\le g\\in L^1(S).\n \\tag{SEC3.a}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|k(s)\\|\\le g(s)\\quad\\text{almost everywhere},\\qquad\n 0\\le g\\in L^1(S).\n \\tag{SEC3.a}\n\\]<\/span>\nEvery such scalar function is integrable. The rule\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\ell\\longmapsto\\int_S\\ell(k(s))\\,ds\n \\tag{SEC3.b}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\ell\\longmapsto\\int_S\\ell(k(s))\\,ds\n \\tag{SEC3.b}\n\\]<\/span>\nis complex linear and bounded on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_*\\)\">\\(E_*\\)<\/span>, with norm at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int_Sg\\)\">\\(\\int_Sg\\)<\/span>. Duality therefore gives a unique element, denoted <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\)\">\\(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\)<\/span>, such that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\ell\\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)\n   =\\int_S\\ell(k(s))\\,ds\n       \\quad(\\ell\\in E_*).\n \\tag{SEC3.c}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\ell\\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)\n   =\\int_S\\ell(k(s))\\,ds\n       \\quad(\\ell\\in E_*).\n \\tag{SEC3.c}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is the weak-star integral. In particular, for a locally Lusin fixed-fiber function with integrable norm,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\left\\|\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right\\|\n     \\le\\int_S\\|k(s)\\|\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC3.d}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\left\\|\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right\\|\n     \\le\\int_S\\|k(s)\\|\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC3.d}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> the duality used here is proved in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6\">CP6<\/a>; for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M(t)\\)\">\\(M(t)\\)<\/span> it is transported by the normal isometric coefficient chart. The integration variable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> does not change the output degree <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>. No addition of elements from different fibers is involved.<\/p>\n<p>Scalar integration and separation by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_*\\)\">\\(E_*\\)<\/span> prove linearity, invariance under almost-everywhere changes, and additivity over measurable pieces. They also prove dominated weak-star convergence: if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell(k_n(s))\\to\\ell(k(s))\\)\">\\(\\ell(k_n(s))\\to\\ell(k(s))\\)<\/span> almost everywhere for each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell\\)\">\\(\\ell\\)<\/span>, with a common integrable bound for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|k_n(s)\\|\\)\">\\(\\|k_n(s)\\|\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|k(s)\\|\\)\">\\(\\|k(s)\\|\\)<\/span>, then the integrals converge weak-star. For this conclusion the scalar null set may depend on the fixed functional being tested.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Normal linear and conjugate-linear transport.<\/strong> A weak-star continuous complex-linear functional on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E=(E_*)^*\\)\">\\(E=(E_*)^*\\)<\/span> belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_*\\)\">\\(E_*\\)<\/span>: continuity controls it by finitely many predual tests, so it vanishes on their common kernel. It factors through their finite-dimensional image and is consequently a linear combination of those tests. This is the same finite-test argument used in CP6.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A:E\\to F\\)\">\\(A:E\\to F\\)<\/span> be a bounded weak-star continuous complex-linear map between specified dual Banach spaces. Its preadjoint is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_*\\lambda=\\lambda\\circ A\\)\">\\(A_*\\lambda=\\lambda\\circ A\\)<\/span>, so (SEC3.c) gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n A\\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)\n       =\\int_S A(k(s))\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC3.e}\n\\]\">\\[\n A\\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)\n       =\\int_S A(k(s))\\,ds.\n \\tag{SEC3.e}\n\\]<\/span>\nHere the right side exists by the bound <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|A(k(s))\\|\\le\\|A\\|g(s)\\)\">\\(\\|A(k(s))\\|\\le\\|A\\|g(s)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The same identity holds when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is bounded, conjugate linear and weak-star continuous. To prove it, define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (A^\\natural\\lambda)(x)=\\overline{\\lambda(Ax)}\n       \\quad(\\lambda\\in F_*,\\ x\\in E).\n \\tag{SEC3.f}\n\\]\">\\[\n (A^\\natural\\lambda)(x)=\\overline{\\lambda(Ax)}\n       \\quad(\\lambda\\in F_*,\\ x\\in E).\n \\tag{SEC3.f}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is a complex-linear weak-star continuous functional of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, hence belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_*\\)\">\\(E_*\\)<\/span>. Applying (SEC3.c) to it and then conjugating the scalar integral gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\lambda\\!\\left(A\\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)\\right)\n &amp;=\\overline{(A^\\natural\\lambda)\n                    \\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)}\\\\\n &amp;=\\int_S\\lambda(A(k(s)))\\,ds.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC3.g}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\lambda\\!\\left(A\\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)\\right)\n &amp;=\\overline{(A^\\natural\\lambda)\n                    \\!\\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)}\\\\\n &amp;=\\int_S\\lambda(A(k(s)))\\,ds.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC3.g}\n\\]<\/span>\nAll <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\in F_*\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\in F_*\\)<\/span> separate the points of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>, proving (SEC3.e) in this case as well. The map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A^\\natural\\)\">\\(A^\\natural\\)<\/span> is conjugate linear in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span>; it is not being called a complex-linear preadjoint.<\/p>\n<p>These maps also preserve local Mackey–Lusin measurability. Indeed, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\subset F_*\\)\">\\(K\\subset F_*\\)<\/span> is absolutely convex and weakly compact, its image under <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_*\\)\">\\(A_*\\)<\/span>, or under <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A^\\natural\\)\">\\(A^\\natural\\)<\/span>, is absolutely convex and weakly compact in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_*\\)\">\\(E_*\\)<\/span>. Weak continuity follows by testing at each fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>; conjugation in (SEC3.f) preserves continuity. The seminorm identity\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n p_K(Ax)=p_{A_*K}(x)\n \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n p_K(Ax)=p_{A^\\natural K}(x)\n \\tag{SEC3.h}\n\\]\">\\[\n p_K(Ax)=p_{A_*K}(x)\n \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n p_K(Ax)=p_{A^\\natural K}(x)\n \\tag{SEC3.h}\n\\]<\/span>\nproves Mackey continuity on the entire space. Compose on each Lusin compact set to get the stated measurability assertion.<\/p>\n<p>The graded adjoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M(t)\\to M(-t)\\)\">\\(M(t)\\to M(-t)\\)<\/span> is a bounded normal conjugate-linear isometry: in a faithful chart it is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\mapsto\\sigma_{-t}^\\varphi(x^*)\\)\">\\(x\\mapsto\\sigma_{-t}^\\varphi(x^*)\\)<\/span>, by <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GRD.html#grd-3\">GRD3<\/a>; the adjoint is ultraweakly continuous by CP6's vector-series formula, and the automorphism is normal. Therefore\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)^*\n       =\\int_S k(s)^*\\,ds\n       \\quad\\text{in }M(-t).\n \\tag{SEC3.i}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\left(\\int_S k(s)\\,ds\\right)^*\n       =\\int_S k(s)^*\\,ds\n       \\quad\\text{in }M(-t).\n \\tag{SEC3.i}\n\\]<\/span>\nFixed coefficient charts, fixed left or right multipliers, and each fixed modular automorphism satisfy the linear transport formula for the same reason. For example a normal functional evaluated at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(axb\\)\">\\(axb\\)<\/span> is again a square-summable vector series, with its vectors changed by the fixed bounded operators <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a,b\\)\">\\(a,b\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Strong measurability on an arbitrary Hilbert space.<\/strong> Represent <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> faithfully and normally on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>; no separability of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is assumed. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k:\\mathbb R\\to M\\)\">\\(k:\\mathbb R\\to M\\)<\/span> be locally Lusin. The countable compact-cover construction of Section 1 supplies compact sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_j\\)\">\\(K_j\\)<\/span> whose union is conull, such that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k|_{K_j}\\)\">\\(k|_{K_j}\\)<\/span> is Mackey continuous. The compact-image norm bound of that section and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GRD.html#grd-5\">GRD4–5<\/a> make each restriction strong-star continuous in this representation. Thus, for every fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in H\\)<\/span>, the two maps\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n s\\longmapsto k(s)\\xi,\\qquad\n s\\longmapsto k(s)^*\\xi\n \\tag{SEC3.j}\n\\]\">\\[\n s\\longmapsto k(s)\\xi,\\qquad\n s\\longmapsto k(s)^*\\xi\n \\tag{SEC3.j}\n\\]<\/span>\nare continuous on each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_j\\)\">\\(K_j\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Each of their compact images in the metric space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is separable: choose a finite <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/n\\)\">\\(1/n\\)<\/span>-net for every positive integer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>. The closed linear span of the resulting countable union of sets is separable. Outside the null complement of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bigcup_jK_j\\)\">\\(\\bigcup_jK_j\\)<\/span>, it contains the entire vector range. The maps are norm-Borel measurable after setting them to zero on that complement: the inverse image of an open subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is a countable union of relatively open subsets of the compact <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_j\\)\">\\(K_j\\)<\/span>, with the Borel complement added if needed. With the original null values restored they are measurable for completed Lebesgue measure.<\/p>\n<p>The strong-measurability proof in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L24.html#oa-flow.grp.vectorintegration\">L24, Section 4<\/a> now applies. In a separable essential range, measurable first-nearby-point choices from a countable dense set, followed by finite truncations, give finite-valued measurable approximants almost everywhere. Hence both functions in (SEC3.j) are strongly measurable even when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> itself is not separable.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int\\|k(s)\\|\\,ds&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(\\int\\|k(s)\\|\\,ds&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>, they have integrable vector norms. L24's Bochner integral is therefore available, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\left(\\int k(s)\\,ds\\right)\\xi\n      &amp;=\\int k(s)\\xi\\,ds,\\\\\n \\left(\\int k(s)\\,ds\\right)^*\\xi\n      &amp;=\\int k(s)^*\\xi\\,ds.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC3.k}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\left(\\int k(s)\\,ds\\right)\\xi\n      &amp;=\\int k(s)\\xi\\,ds,\\\\\n \\left(\\int k(s)\\,ds\\right)^*\\xi\n      &amp;=\\int k(s)^*\\xi\\,ds.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{SEC3.k}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor the first identity, test against each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta\\in H\\)\">\\(\\eta\\in H\\)<\/span>; the vector functional belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span>, so its two scalar integrals agree by (SEC3.c). Hilbert-space separation proves equality. The second follows in the same way, or from (SEC3.i). For a fixed graded fiber first apply its coefficient chart; equivalently use its normal embedding into the core and a faithful normal representation of the core. Thus the comparison respects the canonical fiber integral.<\/p>\n<p>The argument also handles products with the implementing unitaries needed below. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MW.html#oa-flow.mw.4\">faithful-weight implementation theorem<\/a> gives a faithful normal GNS representation with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_s^\\varphi(x)=W_sxW_s^*\\)\">\\(\\sigma_s^\\varphi(x)=W_sxW_s^*\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_s\\)\">\\(W_s\\)<\/span> is strongly continuous. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v(s)\\)\">\\(v(s)\\)<\/span> is continuous on a compact restriction, then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|W_sv(s)-W_{s_0}v(s_0)\\|\n \\le\\|v(s)-v(s_0)\\|+\\|(W_s-W_{s_0})v(s_0)\\|\n \\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SEC3.l}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|W_sv(s)-W_{s_0}v(s_0)\\|\n \\le\\|v(s)-v(s_0)\\|+\\|(W_s-W_{s_0})v(s_0)\\|\n \\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{SEC3.l}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe same holds for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_s^*\\)\">\\(W_s^*\\)<\/span>. Apply this to (SEC3.j) on its compact restrictions. Their countable compact images again prove strong measurability of fields such as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_s^*k(s)^*\\xi\\)\">\\(W_s^*k(s)^*\\xi\\)<\/span>. A bound for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|k(s)\\|\\)\">\\(\\|k(s)\\|\\)<\/span> supplies the corresponding integrable majorant whenever required.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Vector translation continuity.<\/strong> For every strongly measurable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F:\\mathbb R\\to H\\)\">\\(F:\\mathbb R\\to H\\)<\/span> with integrable norm,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\|F(r+h)-F(r)\\|\\,dr\\longrightarrow0\n       \\quad(h\\to0).\n \\tag{SEC3.m}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\|F(r+h)-F(r)\\|\\,dr\\longrightarrow0\n       \\quad(h\\to0).\n \\tag{SEC3.m}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor a finite sum <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S=\\sum_{j=1}^m1_{A_j}\\xi_j\\)\">\\(S=\\sum_{j=1}^m1_{A_j}\\xi_j\\)<\/span>, with the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_j\\)\">\\(A_j\\)<\/span> of finite measure, the left side is at most\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_j\\|\\xi_j\\|\\,|(A_j-h)\\mathbin{\\triangle}A_j|\\)\">\\(\\sum_j\\|\\xi_j\\|\\,|(A_j-h)\\mathbin{\\triangle}A_j|\\)<\/span>.\nEach term tends to zero by the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FF.html#oa-flow.ff.2\">scalar translation proof<\/a>. That proof obtains finite-measure indicator approximation from Lebesgue regularity and bounded intervals. L24 supplies such finite-valued approximants <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> in integral norm for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>. Translation preserves that norm, so\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|F(\\,\\cdot+h)-F\\|_{L^1(H)}\n \\le2\\|F-S\\|_{L^1(H)}\n       +\\|S(\\,\\cdot+h)-S\\|_{L^1(H)}.\n \\tag{SEC3.n}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|F(\\,\\cdot+h)-F\\|_{L^1(H)}\n \\le2\\|F-S\\|_{L^1(H)}\n       +\\|S(\\,\\cdot+h)-S\\|_{L^1(H)}.\n \\tag{SEC3.n}\n\\]<\/span>\nFirst choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span>, then decrease <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span>. This proves (SEC3.m).<\/p>\n<p><strong>The Fubini hypotheses.<\/strong> We will use vector Fubini only for a strongly measurable function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F:\\mathbb R^2\\to H\\)\">\\(F:\\mathbb R^2\\to H\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\|F(r,s)\\|\\,dr\\,ds&lt;\\infty.\n \\tag{SEC3.o}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\|F(r,s)\\|\\,dr\\,ds&lt;\\infty.\n \\tag{SEC3.o}\n\\]<\/span>\nL24, Proposition 4.3 proves that its iterated Bochner integrals exist almost everywhere and equal its product integral. Its sigma compact carrier condition holds on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R^2\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R^2\\)<\/span>. The proof approximates in integral norm by finite-valued functions and uses scalar Fubini on a summable series of their norm differences, so it applies to arbitrary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For operator identities there is a convenient sufficient hypothesis that also controls exceptional sets. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K:\\mathbb R^2\\to M(t)\\)\">\\(K:\\mathbb R^2\\to M(t)\\)<\/span> be locally Mackey–Lusin on bounded rectangles and suppose that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|K(r,s)\\|\\le g(r,s)\\)\">\\(\\|K(r,s)\\|\\le g(r,s)\\)<\/span> almost everywhere, for a measurable integrable scalar <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>. Countably many compact restrictions cover a conull Borel set, by the same decreasing-loss construction as in Section 1. After setting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=0\\)\">\\(K=0\\)<\/span> off this set, each predual pairing is jointly Borel: its restriction to each compact is continuous, and a countable union of the corresponding Borel pieces gives every inverse image. The scalar norm is measurable as well. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-FF.html#scalar-interchange\">scalar Fubini proof<\/a> then gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R^2}K(r,s)\\,dr\\,ds\n =\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}K(r,s)\\,ds\\right)dr\n =\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}K(r,s)\\,dr\\right)ds,\n \\tag{SEC3.p}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_{\\mathbb R^2}K(r,s)\\,dr\\,ds\n =\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}K(r,s)\\,ds\\right)dr\n =\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R}K(r,s)\\,dr\\right)ds,\n \\tag{SEC3.p}\n\\]<\/span>\nwith all three integrals understood weak-star.<\/p>\n<p>To justify the operator statement, the set where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int g(r,s)\\,ds\\)\">\\(\\int g(r,s)\\,ds\\)<\/span> is infinite is one scalar null set, independent of the normal functional. Off it every inner weak-star integral exists by (SEC3.a)–(SEC3.c), since the Borel representative has measurable scalar sections for every functional. Its scalar pairings are measurable functions of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, and its norm is bounded by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int g(r,s)\\,ds\\)\">\\(\\int g(r,s)\\,ds\\)<\/span>. The outer weak-star integral exists using that bound, even without a separate assertion of norm measurability of the inner integral. Testing each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell\\in M(t)_*\\)\">\\(\\ell\\in M(t)_*\\)<\/span> and applying scalar Fubini proves the first equality; reverse <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r,s\\)\">\\(r,s\\)<\/span> for the second. If the original field differs on a product-null set, scalar Fubini first removes the null set of rows or columns on which its section need not be null. This single set again does not depend on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell\\)\">\\(\\ell\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>On each compact restriction, coefficient fields applied to a fixed vector, and their adjoints, are continuous with compact metric image. The argument for (SEC3.j) therefore also proves strong measurability of these joint vector fields. Bound (SEC3.o) follows from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\|\\xi\\|\\)\">\\(g\\|\\xi\\|\\)<\/span>, so (SEC3.p) agrees with the Bochner comparisons whenever they are used.<\/p>\n<p>These statements iterate to further real variables under an integrable scalar norm majorant. For an additional parameter <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span>, if a scalar triple-integral bound makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int\\!\\int g_u(r,s)\\,dr\\,ds\\)\">\\(\\int\\!\\int g_u(r,s)\\,dr\\,ds\\)<\/span> finite outside a null set of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span>, apply (SEC3.p) for each such <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> once local Lusin measurability of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_u\\)\">\\(K_u\\)<\/span> has been proved. This supplies a common parameter exception chosen from the scalar majorant, rather than from an uncountable family of vector or functional tests.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-SEC.md","start_offset":12955,"end_offset":25204,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"6378595e2b234c1387a451a7488b2a247f5a17378705b579e6662d284b61a77a":{"markdown":"# Type III zero factors from a contracting return automorphism\n\n<a id=\"zdc-setting\"><\/a>\n## Theorems and prerequisites\n\nA return transformation records one discrete step of a continuous center flow. The trace records more: when the return roof has height \\(r\\), one step changes the coefficient trace by the central factor \\(e^{-r}\\). The corresponding suspension trace has density \\(e^{-u}\\,du\\) in the height variable. We construct both traces on their entire positive cones and identify the actual regular crossed products.\n\nAll algebras in the two main theorems have separable predual. A type II\\(_\\infty\\) algebra need not be a factor: it is semifinite, has no nonzero abelian projection, and has no nonzero finite central summand. A faithful normal semifinite trace is abbreviated to a faithful n.s.f. trace. The regular convention is\n\\[\n [\\pi_\\alpha(x)\\xi](t)=\\alpha_{-t}(x)\\xi(t),\\qquad\n [\\lambda_s\\xi](t)=\\xi(t-s).\n \\tag{ZDC1}\n\\]\nFor an automorphism the analogous formulas use integer indices. All isomorphisms below are normal and have normal inverses.\n\n**Existence.** Every nonzero type III\\(_0\\) factor \\(M\\) with separable predual has a presentation\n\\[\n M\\cong N\\rtimes_\\alpha\\mathbb Z\n \\tag{ZDC2}\n\\]\nin which \\(N\\) is type II\\(_\\infty\\) with separable predual, \\(Z(N)\\) is nonatomic, \\(\\alpha\\) is centrally ergodic, and a faithful n.s.f. trace \\(\\tau\\) satisfies\n\\[\n \\tau\\circ\\alpha=\\tau_h,\\qquad\n 0<h\\le c1,\\quad 0<c<1,\\quad h\\text{ affiliated with }Z(N).\n \\tag{ZDC3}\n\\]\nIts flow of weights is the suspension of the center transformation with roof \\(-\\log h\\). The equality uses central density calculus, so \\(\\tau_h(x)\\) includes infinite values.\n\n**Converse.** If \\(N,\\alpha,\\tau\\) have these stated properties, or merely the full-cone inequality \\(\\tau\\alpha\\le c\\tau\\) in place of the equality in (ZDC3), then \\(N\\rtimes_\\alpha\\mathbb Z\\) is a type III\\(_0\\) factor. Its flow of weights is the suspension just described.\n\nThe continuous-decomposition input is [CDEC3–4](OA-FLOW-CDEC.md#cdec-3), with the full supported type criterion in [L18, Sections 5–6](OA-FLOW-L18.md#l18-5), and the complete invariant in [MIV1–4](OA-FLOW-MIV.md#miv-1). The return-system input is the [full lifted return system](OA-FLOW-SR.md#sr-cover), with both the [imprimitivity construction](OA-FLOW-SR.md#sr-iw) and the [cocycle-stability construction](OA-FLOW-SR.md#sr-cst), its [normal crossed-product maps and inverses](OA-FLOW-SR.md#sr-cross), [full center and deck action](OA-FLOW-SR.md#sr-center), and [all three coarse-type conclusions](OA-FLOW-SR.md#sr-type). The measurable coordinates are the [signed suspension and product-class theorem](OA-FLOW-L38.md#oa-flow.suspension.cover).","html":"<h1>Type III zero factors from a contracting return automorphism<\/h1>\n<h2>Theorems and prerequisites<\/h2>\n<p>A return transformation records one discrete step of a continuous center flow. The trace records more: when the return roof has height <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, one step changes the coefficient trace by the central factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-r}\\)\">\\(e^{-r}\\)<\/span>. The corresponding suspension trace has density <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-u}\\,du\\)\">\\(e^{-u}\\,du\\)<\/span> in the height variable. We construct both traces on their entire positive cones and identify the actual regular crossed products.<\/p>\n<p>All algebras in the two main theorems have separable predual. A type II<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(_\\infty\\)\">\\(_\\infty\\)<\/span> algebra need not be a factor: it is semifinite, has no nonzero abelian projection, and has no nonzero finite central summand. A faithful normal semifinite trace is abbreviated to a faithful n.s.f. trace. The regular convention is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n [\\pi_\\alpha(x)\\xi](t)=\\alpha_{-t}(x)\\xi(t),\\qquad\n [\\lambda_s\\xi](t)=\\xi(t-s).\n \\tag{ZDC1}\n\\]\">\\[\n [\\pi_\\alpha(x)\\xi](t)=\\alpha_{-t}(x)\\xi(t),\\qquad\n [\\lambda_s\\xi](t)=\\xi(t-s).\n \\tag{ZDC1}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor an automorphism the analogous formulas use integer indices. All isomorphisms below are normal and have normal inverses.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Existence.<\/strong> Every nonzero type III<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(_0\\)\">\\(_0\\)<\/span> factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> with separable predual has a presentation\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n M\\cong N\\rtimes_\\alpha\\mathbb Z\n \\tag{ZDC2}\n\\]\">\\[\n M\\cong N\\rtimes_\\alpha\\mathbb Z\n \\tag{ZDC2}\n\\]<\/span>\nin which <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is type II<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(_\\infty\\)\">\\(_\\infty\\)<\/span> with separable predual, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z(N)\\)\">\\(Z(N)\\)<\/span> is nonatomic, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span> is centrally ergodic, and a faithful n.s.f. trace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span> satisfies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\tau\\circ\\alpha=\\tau_h,\\qquad\n 0&lt;h\\le c1,\\quad 0&lt;c&lt;1,\\quad h\\text{ affiliated with }Z(N).\n \\tag{ZDC3}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\tau\\circ\\alpha=\\tau_h,\\qquad\n 0&lt;h\\le c1,\\quad 0&lt;c&lt;1,\\quad h\\text{ affiliated with }Z(N).\n \\tag{ZDC3}\n\\]<\/span>\nIts flow of weights is the suspension of the center transformation with roof <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-\\log h\\)\">\\(-\\log h\\)<\/span>. The equality uses central density calculus, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau_h(x)\\)\">\\(\\tau_h(x)\\)<\/span> includes infinite values.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Converse.<\/strong> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N,\\alpha,\\tau\\)\">\\(N,\\alpha,\\tau\\)<\/span> have these stated properties, or merely the full-cone inequality <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\alpha\\le c\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\alpha\\le c\\tau\\)<\/span> in place of the equality in (ZDC3), then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\rtimes_\\alpha\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(N\\rtimes_\\alpha\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span> is a type III<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(_0\\)\">\\(_0\\)<\/span> factor. Its flow of weights is the suspension just described.<\/p>\n<p>The continuous-decomposition input is <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CDEC.html#cdec-3\">CDEC3–4<\/a>, with the full supported type criterion in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L18.html#l18-5\">L18, Sections 5–6<\/a>, and the complete invariant in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MIV.html#miv-1\">MIV1–4<\/a>. The return-system input is the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SR.html#sr-cover\">full lifted return system<\/a>, with both the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SR.html#sr-iw\">imprimitivity construction<\/a> and the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SR.html#sr-cst\">cocycle-stability construction<\/a>, its <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SR.html#sr-cross\">normal crossed-product maps and inverses<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SR.html#sr-center\">full center and deck action<\/a>, and <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SR.html#sr-type\">all three coarse-type conclusions<\/a>. The measurable coordinates are the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L38.html#oa-flow.suspension.cover\">signed suspension and product-class theorem<\/a>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-ZDC.md","start_offset":0,"end_offset":2688,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"6344febb9243fdca23e71a0be523fed5f120b8df7172f2555fd629b195b94f61":{"markdown":"<a id=\"crng-checks\"><\/a>\n## 5. Exact consequences and projection coordinates\n\n- The zero orbit maps to zero, and distance to that orbit is exactly \\(\\phi(1)\\).\n- For \\(c=e^{-s}>0\\), equation (OR3) reads\n  \\(\\chi_{e^{-s}\\phi}=e^{-s}\\chi_\\phi\\theta_{-s}\\), with both the mass factor and negative time retained.\n- Equality of two invariants concerns closed orbits. It need not be attained by one unitary. Even on \\(B(\\ell^2)\\), the densities with diagonal lists \\((2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\) and \\((0,2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\) have the same positive eigenvalues but different kernel dimensions.\n- A scalar center has only two projections but has positive normal functionals of every nonnegative mass. The invariant in (OR1) therefore has the required codomain even in type III₁.\n\n<a id=\"crng-projection-coordinates\"><\/a>\n### Central projections as countable coordinates\n\nFix a faithful normal state \\(\\rho\\) on \\(Z\\). Such a state exists: the regular core embeds in \\(M\\bar\\otimes B(L^2(\\mathbb R))\\). The tensor-functional density argument in [CAPP, Section 2](OA-FLOW-CAPP.md#capp-lift), together with the separability of \\(M_*\\) and of the trace-class operators on \\(L^2(\\mathbb R)\\), makes the ambient predual separable. The normal restriction quotients in [CP6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.6) give separability for \\(C_*\\) and \\(Z_*\\). The explicit faithful-state construction in [HC7](OA-FLOW-HC.md#oa-flow.hc.7) then applies.\n\nFor the required separable Hilbert realization, [CR8](OA-FLOW-CR.md#oa-flow.cr.8) sends a countable norm-dense set of positive normal functionals of \\(M\\) to a dense set in its standard cone, since \\(\\|\\xi_f-\\xi_g\\|^2\\le\\|f-g\\|\\). [CR1](OA-FLOW-CR.md#oa-flow.cr.1) proves that the complex span of that cone is the full standard Hilbert space. It is therefore separable, as is the regular core space \\(L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\). In particular its represented center satisfies DC06's separable-Hilbert-space hypothesis.\n\nUse the abelian realization in [DC06](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#realizing-an-abelian-algebra-as-the-diagonal-algebra) and the finite-measure density proof in [DC05](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#a-finite-radon-nikodym-density-from-hilbert-representation). They give a unique nonnegative \\(g_\\phi\\in L^1(Z,\\rho)\\) with\n\\[\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=\\rho(g_\\phi z),\\qquad\n \\rho(g_\\phi)=\\phi(1),\\qquad\n \\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|=\\rho(|g_\\phi-g_\\psi|).\n \\tag{OR7}\n\\]\nThe last equality follows by testing the bounded sign of the real density difference; the reverse inequality is the integral triangle inequality. Faithfulness of \\(\\rho\\) identifies its null sets with the null projections of \\(Z\\).\n\nFor every positive rational \\(r\\), define\n\\[\n p_{\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}(g_\\phi)\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(Z),\\qquad\n \\mathbf p_\\rho(\\phi)=(p_{\\phi,r})_{r\\in\\mathbb Q_{>0}}.\n \\tag{OR8}\n\\]\nThe densities are finite almost everywhere, so their rational strict superlevel sets determine them. Explicitly, outside the union of the countably many null sets where two corresponding cuts differ, distinct finite density values would have a positive rational strictly between them. Thus the coordinate families coincide exactly when \\(g_\\phi=g_\\psi\\), equivalently \\(\\chi_\\phi=\\chi_\\psi\\), equivalently the norm-closed unitary orbits coincide by (OR2). This includes zero and all nonfaithful functionals.\n\nRepresent \\(Z\\) on \\(L^2(Z,\\rho)\\), and give its projection space the strong-operator Borel structure, metrized by \\(d_\\rho(p,q)=\\rho(|p-q|^2)^{1/2}\\). On uniformly bounded multiplication operators, convergence in \\(L^2(\\rho)\\) implies strong convergence: truncate any test vector to a bounded vector, use the \\(L^2\\) bound on that part, then use the common operator bound on the remaining vector tail. Conversely strong convergence tested on the constant vector gives \\(L^2\\) convergence. This also proves the asserted description on the projection space.\n\nEach map \\(\\phi\\mapsto p_{\\phi,r}\\) is Borel. Indeed (OR7) and contraction of \\(\\chi\\) make \\(\\phi\\mapsto g_\\phi\\) continuous from the positive predual norm into \\(L^1(\\rho)\\). Put\n\\[\n F_{n,r}(t)=\\min(1,n(t-r)_+).\n\\]\nFor nonnegative \\(g,h\\),\n\\(\\|F_{n,r}(g)-F_{n,r}(h)\\|_2^2\\le n\\|g-h\\|_1\\),\nsince the function has Lipschitz constant \\(n\\) and its values lie in \\([0,1]\\). Hence these bounded-operator maps are continuous. Dominated convergence gives \\(F_{n,r}(g)\\to1_{(r,\\infty)}(g)\\) strongly. A pointwise limit of Borel maps into the metric space of positive contractions with metric \\(d_\\rho\\) is Borel: distance to every closed set is the limit of the corresponding distances, and open sets can be tested by positive distance to their complements. The countable product map in (OR8) is consequently Borel.\n\nThe coordinate projections from the **full positive predual** generate \\(Z\\). Let \\(B\\) be the von Neumann algebra they generate. All \\(g_\\phi\\) are measurable for \\(B\\), because their rational spectral cuts lie in \\(B\\). Section 4 proves that the complex linear span of the \\(\\chi_\\phi\\) is norm dense in \\(Z_*\\); by (OR7), the same density holds for the span of the \\(g_\\phi\\) in \\(L^1(\\rho)\\). Given a selfadjoint \\(z\\in Z\\), \\(\\|z\\|\\le K\\), approximate it in \\(L^1\\) by real linear combinations of the \\(g_\\phi\\), taking real parts of complex approximations. Clip those combinations to \\([-K,K]\\). The clipped functions belong to \\(B\\), and clipping cannot increase their pointwise distance from \\(z\\). Thus they converge to \\(z\\) in \\(L^1\\), hence in \\(L^2\\) by the common bound, and strongly by the preceding vector-tail argument. Strong closedness gives \\(z\\in B\\), proving \\(B=Z\\).\n\n### Scalar covariance and the role of the domain\n\nFor real \\(s\\), let \\(d_s=d(\\rho\\theta_s)/d\\rho\\). It is positive and finite almost everywhere, because both states are faithful. For a nonnegative density \\(g\\), first use bounded cutoffs and then monotone convergence to obtain\n\\[\n (g\\rho)\\theta_s(z)=\\rho\\bigl(d_s\\,\\theta_{-s}(g)\\,z\\bigr)\n \\qquad(z\\in Z_+).\n\\]\nIndeed the right side is \\((\\rho\\theta_s)(\\theta_{-s}(g)z)=\\rho(g\\theta_s(z))\\). Formula (OR3) therefore becomes\n\\[\n g_{c\\phi}=c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi),\\qquad\n p_{c\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}\n       \\bigl(c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi)\\bigr)\n \\quad(c>0).\n \\tag{OR9}\n\\]\nThe products are products of commuting measurable functions, and the displayed integral proves their integrability. At \\(c=0\\) every coordinate is zero. For a state \\(\\phi\\), the functional \\(e^{-s}\\phi\\) has mass \\(e^{-s}\\), so it belongs to the state space only when \\(s=0\\). The positive-predual domain and the density factor in (OR9) are therefore part of the statement.\n\nState inputs alone need not generate the center by these coordinates. For \\(M=B(\\ell^2)\\), a trace-class state density has every eigenvalue at most one. Formula (OR6) makes \\(\\chi_\\phi\\), and hence \\(g_\\phi\\), vanish on the half-line \\(q\\le0\\). All its positive spectral cuts vanish there. Their generated unital algebra restricts to scalars on that half-line, whereas \\(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\) contains nonconstant functions supported there. Thus the generation theorem just proved genuinely requires the full positive domain.\n\nThe single spectral projection \\(e_\\phi\\) of (OR1) is a different object: it lies in \\(C\\) and need not be central or constant on a unitary orbit. In the same type I example, the vector state at a basis vector \\(e_j\\) has\n\\(e_\\phi=|e_j\\rangle\\langle e_j|\\otimes1_{(0,\\infty)}(q)\\).\nMatrix units show this projection is noncentral; exchanging two basis vectors changes it within one state orbit. The central functional in (OR1) is unchanged.\n\nFinally, no map into \\(\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\) alone can separate all positive-functional orbits for every infinite factor. In type III₁, \\(Z=\\mathbb C\\) has only two projections, while (OR5) gives distinct orbits for any three distinct positive masses. The family (OR8) has the stated separation property. Its countable-coordinate assertion is distinct from a single-projection coding of the mass-one orbit space.\n\n<a id=\"crng-single-projection\"><\/a>\n### One central projection for each closed state orbit\n\nThere does exist a Borel map\n\\[\n p:S_*(M)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\n \\tag{OR10}\n\\]\nsuch that \\(p(\\phi)=p(\\psi)\\) exactly when the closed state orbits coincide, and \\(p(S_*(M))\\) generates \\(Z\\). The following explicit coding proves existence. It is a choice of labels; its projections need not be the spectral projections in (OR1) or (OR8).\n\nFirst \\(Z\\) is either scalar or diffuse. [CORE8](OA-FLOW-CORE.md#core-8) proves \\(Z^\\theta=\\mathbb C1\\) because \\(M\\) is a factor. If \\(e\\) is a nonzero minimal projection of \\(Z\\), each \\(\\theta_s(e)\\) is either \\(e\\) or is orthogonal to \\(e\\). In the latter case\n\\(\\rho(|\\theta_s(e)-e|^2)\\ge\\rho(e)>0\\).\nContinuity makes \\(\\theta_s(e)=e\\) for all sufficiently small \\(s\\). Dividing any real \\(s\\) into sufficiently small equal increments proves invariance for every \\(s\\). Ergodicity gives \\(e=1\\), and minimality of \\(1\\) means \\(Z=\\mathbb C1\\). In this scalar case (OR2) makes all states have the same closed orbit, so \\(p(\\phi)=1\\) proves (OR10) and generation.\n\nSuppose now \\(Z\\) is diffuse. DC06 supplies a bounded positive selfadjoint generator \\(h\\) of \\(Z\\). Its spectral probability measure\n\\(\\mu(E)=\\rho(1_E(h))\\) has no atoms: a nonzero projection \\(1_{\\{a\\}}(h)\\) would be minimal in \\(Z=\\{h\\}''\\). Thus the distribution\n\\(F(a)=\\mu((-\\infty,a])\\) is continuous. For \\(0<t<1\\), put\n\\[\n q_t=\\inf\\{a:F(a)\\ge t\\},\\qquad P_t=1_{(-\\infty,q_t]}(h).\n\\]\nThe bounded support and continuity of \\(F\\) give \\(F(q_t)=t\\), so \\(\\rho(P_t)=t\\). The projections are nested and\n\\(d_\\rho(P_t,P_v)^2=|t-v|\\); hence \\(t\\mapsto P_t\\) is continuous and injective. They generate \\(Z\\) even if only rational parameters are retained. Indeed\n\\[\n 1_{(-\\infty,a]}(h)\n =\\bigvee_{\\substack{t\\in\\mathbb Q\\\\0<t<F(a)}} P_t .\n\\]\nThe right side is below the left and has the same \\(\\rho\\)-value \\(F(a)\\), by normality; faithfulness proves equality. The formula includes the cases \\(F(a)=0\\) and \\(F(a)=1\\). All spectral projections of the generator therefore belong to the generated algebra.\n\nWe need a sequence \\((\\chi_n)\\) of distinct invariants of states. In type I∞, take trace-class densities with the two positive eigenvalues\n\\(1/2+1/(n+2)\\) and \\(1/2-1/(n+2)\\), \\(n\\ge1\\); (OR6) separates their orbits. In type II∞ or III, the unit-mass slice of \\(P_\\theta\\) is convex and is exactly the state image by (OR4) and mass preservation. It cannot be a singleton: then the full cone would be a single ray, and Section 4's dense-span conclusion would make \\(Z_*\\), hence \\(Z\\), one-dimensional. Take two distinct points in that slice and their convex combinations with coefficients \\(1/(n+1)\\). These give the required sequence; every \\(\\chi_n\\) is realized by a state.\n\nChoose a countable norm base \\((B_j)\\) for the separable metric space \\(Z_*\\), using rational-radius balls about a countable dense set. Membership in these balls separates points. Let \\(a_j=1\\) when \\(j\\) is a power of two and \\(a_j=0\\) otherwise, and set\n\\[\n b(\\chi)=\\sum_{j\\ge1}\n \\left(3^{-(2j-1)}1_{B_j}(\\chi)+3^{-2j}a_j\\right).\n\\]\nThe series is uniformly convergent, so \\(b\\) is Borel. The first differing ternary digit dominates the remaining tail, hence \\(b\\) is injective. Its values lie in \\((0,1)\\) and are irrational. To verify the latter assertion, a rational number has an eventually periodic base-three expansion, by the finite-remainder long-division algorithm; the terminating expansion has the same property. Our expansion has only digits zero and one, and its even-position subsequence is \\((a_j)\\), which is not eventually periodic because it has infinitely many ones and unbounded gaps between them. Thus this expansion cannot represent a rational number.\n\nEnumerate the rational numbers in \\((0,1)\\) without repetition as \\((t_n)\\). Modify the code only at the selected invariants:\n\\[\n \\beta(\\chi)=\n \\begin{cases}\n t_n,&\\chi=\\chi_n,\\\\\n b(\\chi),&\\chi\\notin\\{\\chi_n:n\\ge1\\}.\n \\end{cases}\n\\]\nSingletons are norm closed, so the countable modification is Borel. It stays injective: the exceptional values are distinct rationals, and every other value is irrational. Now define\n\\[\n p(\\phi)=P_{\\beta(\\chi_\\phi)} .\n \\tag{OR11}\n\\]\nThis is Borel by the continuity of \\(\\phi\\mapsto\\chi_\\phi\\) and \\(t\\mapsto P_t\\). Injectivity of the two scalar codes and (OR2) prove exactly the equivalence in (OR10). The state image contains every \\(P_{t_n}\\), since every \\(\\chi_n\\) is realized. The rational-parameter argument proves that this image generates \\(Z\\).\n\n<a id=\"crng-scaling-extension\"><\/a>\n### Extend the state labels before applying scalar multiplication\n\nDefine a map on all positive normal functionals by\n\\[\n \\widehat p(0)=0,\\qquad\n \\widehat p(\\phi)=\n \\theta_{-\\log m}\\bigl(p(\\phi/m)\\bigr),\\qquad m=\\phi(1)>0.\n \\tag{OR12}\n\\]\nIt agrees with \\(p\\) on states. The map is Borel, including at zero. Mass and normalization are continuous on the punctured positive cone. For completeness the flow is jointly continuous on \\(\\mathbb R\\times\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\): if \\(s_n\\to s\\) and \\(e_n\\to e\\) in \\(d_\\rho\\), then\n\\[\n \\rho\\theta_{s_n}(|e_n-e|^2)\n \\le\\|\\rho\\theta_{s_n}-\\rho\\theta_s\\|\n       +\\rho\\theta_s(|e_n-e|^2)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThe first term tends to zero by the predual norm-continuity proof in [CAPP's resolvent section](OA-FLOW-CAPP.md#capp-resolvent). For the second, write \\(\\rho\\theta_s=d_s\\rho\\), truncate \\(d_s\\), and use \\(0\\le|e_n-e|^2\\le1\\). Pointwise continuity of \\(s\\mapsto\\theta_s(e)\\), followed by the triangle inequality, proves joint continuity. Thus (OR12) is a composition of Borel maps on the punctured cone, with the Borel singleton zero handled separately.\n\nFor every real \\(s\\) and every positive normal \\(\\phi\\), direct substitution gives\n\\[\n \\widehat p(e^{-s}\\phi)=\\theta_s(\\widehat p(\\phi)).\n \\tag{OR13}\n\\]\nFor nonzero \\(\\phi\\), its normalized state is unchanged by this scaling, while its new mass is \\(e^{-s}m\\); the group parameter in (OR12) becomes \\(s-\\log m\\). For zero both sides are zero. This is the scalar-equivariant projection statement with a domain on which both sides are defined.\n\nOnly the state restriction was asserted to separate orbits by a projection alone. On the whole positive cone the pair\n\\((\\phi(1),\\widehat p(\\phi))\\) separates closed orbits: equal positive masses let us undo the same \\(\\theta_{-\\log m}\\) and apply the state result; mass zero forces both functionals to be zero. Conversely equal closed positive orbits have equal masses and equal normalized closed state orbits. In the scalar-center case, \\(\\widehat p\\) is simply one on every nonzero functional, illustrating why that additional mass coordinate cannot be discarded.\n\nA normal conjugacy of center flows transports these projections to any other normal flow chart. It preserves their equality and generated von Neumann algebra. It also preserves the bounded strong Borel structure: the defining normal-positive-functional seminorms pull back along the isomorphism and its normal inverse. Intertwining the two actions transports (OR13) with the same parameter and sign.\n\nThe human source is U. Haagerup and E. Størmer, [*Equivalence of Normal States on von Neumann Algebras and the Flow of Weights*](https://www.isibang.ac.in/~soumyashant/misc/collected-works-of-haagerup/1990s/1990_Equivalence_of_normal_states_on_von_Neumann_algebras_and_the_flow_of_weights.pdf), Advances in Mathematics 83 (1990), 180–262, especially §§3–8. This theorem retains the separable-predual infinite-factor scope; it does not assert the paper's further arbitrary-cardinality extension.","html":"<h2>5. Exact consequences and projection coordinates<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The zero orbit maps to zero, and distance to that orbit is exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi(1)\\)\">\\(\\phi(1)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=e^{-s}&gt;0\\)\">\\(c=e^{-s}&gt;0\\)<\/span>, equation (OR3) reads\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_{e^{-s}\\phi}=e^{-s}\\chi_\\phi\\theta_{-s}\\)\">\\(\\chi_{e^{-s}\\phi}=e^{-s}\\chi_\\phi\\theta_{-s}\\)<\/span>, with both the mass factor and negative time retained.<\/li>\n<li>Equality of two invariants concerns closed orbits. It need not be attained by one unitary. Even on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(\\ell^2)\\)\">\\(B(\\ell^2)\\)<\/span>, the densities with diagonal lists <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\)\">\\((2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((0,2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\)\">\\((0,2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\)<\/span> have the same positive eigenvalues but different kernel dimensions.<\/li>\n<li>A scalar center has only two projections but has positive normal functionals of every nonnegative mass. The invariant in (OR1) therefore has the required codomain even in type III₁.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>Central projections as countable coordinates<\/h3>\n<p>Fix a faithful normal state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>. Such a state exists: the regular core embeds in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\bar\\otimes B(L^2(\\mathbb R))\\)\">\\(M\\bar\\otimes B(L^2(\\mathbb R))\\)<\/span>. The tensor-functional density argument in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CAPP.html#capp-lift\">CAPP, Section 2<\/a>, together with the separability of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span> and of the trace-class operators on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(\\mathbb R)\\)\">\\(L^2(\\mathbb R)\\)<\/span>, makes the ambient predual separable. The normal restriction quotients in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6\">CP6<\/a> give separability for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_*\\)\">\\(C_*\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z_*\\)\">\\(Z_*\\)<\/span>. The explicit faithful-state construction in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-HC.html#oa-flow.hc.7\">HC7<\/a> then applies.<\/p>\n<p>For the required separable Hilbert realization, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CR.html#oa-flow.cr.8\">CR8<\/a> sends a countable norm-dense set of positive normal functionals of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> to a dense set in its standard cone, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\xi_f-\\xi_g\\|^2\\le\\|f-g\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\xi_f-\\xi_g\\|^2\\le\\|f-g\\|\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CR.html#oa-flow.cr.1\">CR1<\/a> proves that the complex span of that cone is the full standard Hilbert space. It is therefore separable, as is the regular core space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\)\">\\(L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\)<\/span>. In particular its represented center satisfies DC06's separable-Hilbert-space hypothesis.<\/p>\n<p>Use the abelian realization in <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#realizing-an-abelian-algebra-as-the-diagonal-algebra\">DC06<\/a> and the finite-measure density proof in <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#a-finite-radon-nikodym-density-from-hilbert-representation\">DC05<\/a>. They give a unique nonnegative <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi\\in L^1(Z,\\rho)\\)\">\\(g_\\phi\\in L^1(Z,\\rho)\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=\\rho(g_\\phi z),\\qquad\n \\rho(g_\\phi)=\\phi(1),\\qquad\n \\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|=\\rho(|g_\\phi-g_\\psi|).\n \\tag{OR7}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=\\rho(g_\\phi z),\\qquad\n \\rho(g_\\phi)=\\phi(1),\\qquad\n \\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|=\\rho(|g_\\phi-g_\\psi|).\n \\tag{OR7}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe last equality follows by testing the bounded sign of the real density difference; the reverse inequality is the integral triangle inequality. Faithfulness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> identifies its null sets with the null projections of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For every positive rational <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n p_{\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}(g_\\phi)\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(Z),\\qquad\n \\mathbf p_\\rho(\\phi)=(p_{\\phi,r})_{r\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}}.\n \\tag{OR8}\n\\]\">\\[\n p_{\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}(g_\\phi)\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(Z),\\qquad\n \\mathbf p_\\rho(\\phi)=(p_{\\phi,r})_{r\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}}.\n \\tag{OR8}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe densities are finite almost everywhere, so their rational strict superlevel sets determine them. Explicitly, outside the union of the countably many null sets where two corresponding cuts differ, distinct finite density values would have a positive rational strictly between them. Thus the coordinate families coincide exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi=g_\\psi\\)\">\\(g_\\phi=g_\\psi\\)<\/span>, equivalently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_\\phi=\\chi_\\psi\\)\">\\(\\chi_\\phi=\\chi_\\psi\\)<\/span>, equivalently the norm-closed unitary orbits coincide by (OR2). This includes zero and all nonfaithful functionals.<\/p>\n<p>Represent <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(Z,\\rho)\\)\">\\(L^2(Z,\\rho)\\)<\/span>, and give its projection space the strong-operator Borel structure, metrized by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\rho(p,q)=\\rho(|p-q|^2)^{1/2}\\)\">\\(d_\\rho(p,q)=\\rho(|p-q|^2)^{1/2}\\)<\/span>. On uniformly bounded multiplication operators, convergence in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(\\rho)\\)\">\\(L^2(\\rho)\\)<\/span> implies strong convergence: truncate any test vector to a bounded vector, use the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span> bound on that part, then use the common operator bound on the remaining vector tail. Conversely strong convergence tested on the constant vector gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span> convergence. This also proves the asserted description on the projection space.<\/p>\n<p>Each map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\mapsto p_{\\phi,r}\\)\">\\(\\phi\\mapsto p_{\\phi,r}\\)<\/span> is Borel. Indeed (OR7) and contraction of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi\\)\">\\(\\chi\\)<\/span> make <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\mapsto g_\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\mapsto g_\\phi\\)<\/span> continuous from the positive predual norm into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1(\\rho)\\)\">\\(L^1(\\rho)\\)<\/span>. Put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n F_{n,r}(t)=\\min(1,n(t-r)_+).\n\\]\">\\[\n F_{n,r}(t)=\\min(1,n(t-r)_+).\n\\]<\/span>\nFor nonnegative <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g,h\\)\">\\(g,h\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|F_{n,r}(g)-F_{n,r}(h)\\|_2^2\\le n\\|g-h\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|F_{n,r}(g)-F_{n,r}(h)\\|_2^2\\le n\\|g-h\\|_1\\)<\/span>,\nsince the function has Lipschitz constant <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> and its values lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([0,1]\\)\">\\([0,1]\\)<\/span>. Hence these bounded-operator maps are continuous. Dominated convergence gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_{n,r}(g)\\to1_{(r,\\infty)}(g)\\)\">\\(F_{n,r}(g)\\to1_{(r,\\infty)}(g)\\)<\/span> strongly. A pointwise limit of Borel maps into the metric space of positive contractions with metric <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\rho\\)\">\\(d_\\rho\\)<\/span> is Borel: distance to every closed set is the limit of the corresponding distances, and open sets can be tested by positive distance to their complements. The countable product map in (OR8) is consequently Borel.<\/p>\n<p>The coordinate projections from the <strong>full positive predual<\/strong> generate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> be the von Neumann algebra they generate. All <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi\\)\">\\(g_\\phi\\)<\/span> are measurable for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>, because their rational spectral cuts lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>. Section 4 proves that the complex linear span of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_\\phi\\)\">\\(\\chi_\\phi\\)<\/span> is norm dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z_*\\)\">\\(Z_*\\)<\/span>; by (OR7), the same density holds for the span of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi\\)\">\\(g_\\phi\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1(\\rho)\\)\">\\(L^1(\\rho)\\)<\/span>. Given a selfadjoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in Z\\)\">\\(z\\in Z\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|z\\|\\le K\\)\">\\(\\|z\\|\\le K\\)<\/span>, approximate it in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1\\)\">\\(L^1\\)<\/span> by real linear combinations of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi\\)\">\\(g_\\phi\\)<\/span>, taking real parts of complex approximations. Clip those combinations to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([-K,K]\\)\">\\([-K,K]\\)<\/span>. The clipped functions belong to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>, and clipping cannot increase their pointwise distance from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span>. Thus they converge to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1\\)\">\\(L^1\\)<\/span>, hence in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span> by the common bound, and strongly by the preceding vector-tail argument. Strong closedness gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in B\\)\">\\(z\\in B\\)<\/span>, proving <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=Z\\)\">\\(B=Z\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<h3>Scalar covariance and the role of the domain<\/h3>\n<p>For real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_s=d(\\rho\\theta_s)/d\\rho\\)\">\\(d_s=d(\\rho\\theta_s)/d\\rho\\)<\/span>. It is positive and finite almost everywhere, because both states are faithful. For a nonnegative density <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>, first use bounded cutoffs and then monotone convergence to obtain\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (g\\rho)\\theta_s(z)=\\rho\\bigl(d_s\\,\\theta_{-s}(g)\\,z\\bigr)\n \\qquad(z\\in Z_+).\n\\]\">\\[\n (g\\rho)\\theta_s(z)=\\rho\\bigl(d_s\\,\\theta_{-s}(g)\\,z\\bigr)\n \\qquad(z\\in Z_+).\n\\]<\/span>\nIndeed the right side is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\rho\\theta_s)(\\theta_{-s}(g)z)=\\rho(g\\theta_s(z))\\)\">\\((\\rho\\theta_s)(\\theta_{-s}(g)z)=\\rho(g\\theta_s(z))\\)<\/span>. Formula (OR3) therefore becomes\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n g_{c\\phi}=c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi),\\qquad\n p_{c\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}\n       \\bigl(c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi)\\bigr)\n \\quad(c&gt;0).\n \\tag{OR9}\n\\]\">\\[\n g_{c\\phi}=c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi),\\qquad\n p_{c\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}\n       \\bigl(c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi)\\bigr)\n \\quad(c&gt;0).\n \\tag{OR9}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe products are products of commuting measurable functions, and the displayed integral proves their integrability. At <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=0\\)\">\\(c=0\\)<\/span> every coordinate is zero. For a state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\)<\/span>, the functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-s}\\phi\\)\">\\(e^{-s}\\phi\\)<\/span> has mass <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-s}\\)\">\\(e^{-s}\\)<\/span>, so it belongs to the state space only when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s=0\\)\">\\(s=0\\)<\/span>. The positive-predual domain and the density factor in (OR9) are therefore part of the statement.<\/p>\n<p>State inputs alone need not generate the center by these coordinates. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=B(\\ell^2)\\)\">\\(M=B(\\ell^2)\\)<\/span>, a trace-class state density has every eigenvalue at most one. Formula (OR6) makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_\\phi\\)\">\\(\\chi_\\phi\\)<\/span>, and hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi\\)\">\\(g_\\phi\\)<\/span>, vanish on the half-line <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\le0\\)\">\\(q\\le0\\)<\/span>. All its positive spectral cuts vanish there. Their generated unital algebra restricts to scalars on that half-line, whereas <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\)\">\\(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\)<\/span> contains nonconstant functions supported there. Thus the generation theorem just proved genuinely requires the full positive domain.<\/p>\n<p>The single spectral projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_\\phi\\)\">\\(e_\\phi\\)<\/span> of (OR1) is a different object: it lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> and need not be central or constant on a unitary orbit. In the same type I example, the vector state at a basis vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_j\\)\">\\(e_j\\)<\/span> has\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_\\phi=|e_j\\rangle\\langle e_j|\\otimes1_{(0,\\infty)}(q)\\)\">\\(e_\\phi=|e_j\\rangle\\langle e_j|\\otimes1_{(0,\\infty)}(q)\\)<\/span>.\nMatrix units show this projection is noncentral; exchanging two basis vectors changes it within one state orbit. The central functional in (OR1) is unchanged.<\/p>\n<p>Finally, no map into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\)<\/span> alone can separate all positive-functional orbits for every infinite factor. In type III₁, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z=\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(Z=\\mathbb C\\)<\/span> has only two projections, while (OR5) gives distinct orbits for any three distinct positive masses. The family (OR8) has the stated separation property. Its countable-coordinate assertion is distinct from a single-projection coding of the mass-one orbit space.<\/p>\n<h3>One central projection for each closed state orbit<\/h3>\n<p>There does exist a Borel map\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n p:S_*(M)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\n \\tag{OR10}\n\\]\">\\[\n p:S_*(M)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\n \\tag{OR10}\n\\]<\/span>\nsuch that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(\\phi)=p(\\psi)\\)\">\\(p(\\phi)=p(\\psi)\\)<\/span> exactly when the closed state orbits coincide, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(S_*(M))\\)\">\\(p(S_*(M))\\)<\/span> generates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>. The following explicit coding proves existence. It is a choice of labels; its projections need not be the spectral projections in (OR1) or (OR8).<\/p>\n<p>First <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span> is either scalar or diffuse. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CORE.html#core-8\">CORE8<\/a> proves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z^\\theta=\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(Z^\\theta=\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is a factor. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span> is a nonzero minimal projection of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>, each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_s(e)\\)\">\\(\\theta_s(e)\\)<\/span> is either <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span> or is orthogonal to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span>. In the latter case\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(|\\theta_s(e)-e|^2)\\ge\\rho(e)&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\rho(|\\theta_s(e)-e|^2)\\ge\\rho(e)&gt;0\\)<\/span>.\nContinuity makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_s(e)=e\\)\">\\(\\theta_s(e)=e\\)<\/span> for all sufficiently small <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>. Dividing any real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> into sufficiently small equal increments proves invariance for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>. Ergodicity gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e=1\\)\">\\(e=1\\)<\/span>, and minimality of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> means <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z=\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(Z=\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span>. In this scalar case (OR2) makes all states have the same closed orbit, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(\\phi)=1\\)\">\\(p(\\phi)=1\\)<\/span> proves (OR10) and generation.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose now <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span> is diffuse. DC06 supplies a bounded positive selfadjoint generator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>. Its spectral probability measure\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu(E)=\\rho(1_E(h))\\)\">\\(\\mu(E)=\\rho(1_E(h))\\)<\/span> has no atoms: a nonzero projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1_{\\{a\\}}(h)\\)\">\\(1_{\\{a\\}}(h)\\)<\/span> would be minimal in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z=\\{h\\}&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(Z=\\{h\\}&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>. Thus the distribution\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(a)=\\mu((-\\infty,a])\\)\">\\(F(a)=\\mu((-\\infty,a])\\)<\/span> is continuous. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\)\">\\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\)<\/span>, put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n q_t=\\inf\\{a:F(a)\\ge t\\},\\qquad P_t=1_{(-\\infty,q_t]}(h).\n\\]\">\\[\n q_t=\\inf\\{a:F(a)\\ge t\\},\\qquad P_t=1_{(-\\infty,q_t]}(h).\n\\]<\/span>\nThe bounded support and continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(q_t)=t\\)\">\\(F(q_t)=t\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(P_t)=t\\)\">\\(\\rho(P_t)=t\\)<\/span>. The projections are nested and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\rho(P_t,P_v)^2=|t-v|\\)\">\\(d_\\rho(P_t,P_v)^2=|t-v|\\)<\/span>; hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\mapsto P_t\\)\">\\(t\\mapsto P_t\\)<\/span> is continuous and injective. They generate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span> even if only rational parameters are retained. Indeed\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n 1_{(-\\infty,a]}(h)\n =\\bigvee_{\\substack{t\\in\\mathbb Q\\\\0&lt;t&lt;F(a)}} P_t .\n\\]\">\\[\n 1_{(-\\infty,a]}(h)\n =\\bigvee_{\\substack{t\\in\\mathbb Q\\\\0&lt;t&lt;F(a)}} P_t .\n\\]<\/span>\nThe right side is below the left and has the same <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span>-value <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(a)\\)\">\\(F(a)\\)<\/span>, by normality; faithfulness proves equality. The formula includes the cases <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(a)=0\\)\">\\(F(a)=0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(a)=1\\)\">\\(F(a)=1\\)<\/span>. All spectral projections of the generator therefore belong to the generated algebra.<\/p>\n<p>We need a sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\chi_n)\\)\">\\((\\chi_n)\\)<\/span> of distinct invariants of states. In type I∞, take trace-class densities with the two positive eigenvalues\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/2+1/(n+2)\\)\">\\(1/2+1/(n+2)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/2-1/(n+2)\\)\">\\(1/2-1/(n+2)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\ge1\\)\">\\(n\\ge1\\)<\/span>; (OR6) separates their orbits. In type II∞ or III, the unit-mass slice of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_\\theta\\)\">\\(P_\\theta\\)<\/span> is convex and is exactly the state image by (OR4) and mass preservation. It cannot be a singleton: then the full cone would be a single ray, and Section 4's dense-span conclusion would make <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z_*\\)\">\\(Z_*\\)<\/span>, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>, one-dimensional. Take two distinct points in that slice and their convex combinations with coefficients <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/(n+1)\\)\">\\(1/(n+1)\\)<\/span>. These give the required sequence; every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_n\\)\">\\(\\chi_n\\)<\/span> is realized by a state.<\/p>\n<p>Choose a countable norm base <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((B_j)\\)\">\\((B_j)\\)<\/span> for the separable metric space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z_*\\)\">\\(Z_*\\)<\/span>, using rational-radius balls about a countable dense set. Membership in these balls separates points. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_j=1\\)\">\\(a_j=1\\)<\/span> when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span> is a power of two and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_j=0\\)\">\\(a_j=0\\)<\/span> otherwise, and set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n b(\\chi)=\\sum_{j\\ge1}\n \\left(3^{-(2j-1)}1_{B_j}(\\chi)+3^{-2j}a_j\\right).\n\\]\">\\[\n b(\\chi)=\\sum_{j\\ge1}\n \\left(3^{-(2j-1)}1_{B_j}(\\chi)+3^{-2j}a_j\\right).\n\\]<\/span>\nThe series is uniformly convergent, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span> is Borel. The first differing ternary digit dominates the remaining tail, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span> is injective. Its values lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((0,1)\\)\">\\((0,1)\\)<\/span> and are irrational. To verify the latter assertion, a rational number has an eventually periodic base-three expansion, by the finite-remainder long-division algorithm; the terminating expansion has the same property. Our expansion has only digits zero and one, and its even-position subsequence is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((a_j)\\)\">\\((a_j)\\)<\/span>, which is not eventually periodic because it has infinitely many ones and unbounded gaps between them. Thus this expansion cannot represent a rational number.<\/p>\n<p>Enumerate the rational numbers in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((0,1)\\)\">\\((0,1)\\)<\/span> without repetition as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((t_n)\\)\">\\((t_n)\\)<\/span>. Modify the code only at the selected invariants:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\beta(\\chi)=\n \\begin{cases}\n t_n,&amp;\\chi=\\chi_n,\\\\\n b(\\chi),&amp;\\chi\\notin\\{\\chi_n:n\\ge1\\}.\n \\end{cases}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\beta(\\chi)=\n \\begin{cases}\n t_n,&amp;\\chi=\\chi_n,\\\\\n b(\\chi),&amp;\\chi\\notin\\{\\chi_n:n\\ge1\\}.\n \\end{cases}\n\\]<\/span>\nSingletons are norm closed, so the countable modification is Borel. It stays injective: the exceptional values are distinct rationals, and every other value is irrational. Now define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n p(\\phi)=P_{\\beta(\\chi_\\phi)} .\n \\tag{OR11}\n\\]\">\\[\n p(\\phi)=P_{\\beta(\\chi_\\phi)} .\n \\tag{OR11}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is Borel by the continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\mapsto\\chi_\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\mapsto\\chi_\\phi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\mapsto P_t\\)\">\\(t\\mapsto P_t\\)<\/span>. Injectivity of the two scalar codes and (OR2) prove exactly the equivalence in (OR10). The state image contains every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_{t_n}\\)\">\\(P_{t_n}\\)<\/span>, since every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_n\\)\">\\(\\chi_n\\)<\/span> is realized. The rational-parameter argument proves that this image generates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<h3>Extend the state labels before applying scalar multiplication<\/h3>\n<p>Define a map on all positive normal functionals by\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widehat p(0)=0,\\qquad\n \\widehat p(\\phi)=\n \\theta_{-\\log m}\\bigl(p(\\phi/m)\\bigr),\\qquad m=\\phi(1)&gt;0.\n \\tag{OR12}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widehat p(0)=0,\\qquad\n \\widehat p(\\phi)=\n \\theta_{-\\log m}\\bigl(p(\\phi/m)\\bigr),\\qquad m=\\phi(1)&gt;0.\n \\tag{OR12}\n\\]<\/span>\nIt agrees with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> on states. The map is Borel, including at zero. Mass and normalization are continuous on the punctured positive cone. For completeness the flow is jointly continuous on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R\\times\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R\\times\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\)<\/span>: if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_n\\to s\\)\">\\(s_n\\to s\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_n\\to e\\)\">\\(e_n\\to e\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\rho\\)\">\\(d_\\rho\\)<\/span>, then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\rho\\theta_{s_n}(|e_n-e|^2)\n \\le\\|\\rho\\theta_{s_n}-\\rho\\theta_s\\|\n       +\\rho\\theta_s(|e_n-e|^2)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\rho\\theta_{s_n}(|e_n-e|^2)\n \\le\\|\\rho\\theta_{s_n}-\\rho\\theta_s\\|\n       +\\rho\\theta_s(|e_n-e|^2)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe first term tends to zero by the predual norm-continuity proof in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CAPP.html#capp-resolvent\">CAPP's resolvent section<\/a>. For the second, write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\theta_s=d_s\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\theta_s=d_s\\rho\\)<\/span>, truncate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_s\\)\">\\(d_s\\)<\/span>, and use <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\le|e_n-e|^2\\le1\\)\">\\(0\\le|e_n-e|^2\\le1\\)<\/span>. Pointwise continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\mapsto\\theta_s(e)\\)\">\\(s\\mapsto\\theta_s(e)\\)<\/span>, followed by the triangle inequality, proves joint continuity. Thus (OR12) is a composition of Borel maps on the punctured cone, with the Borel singleton zero handled separately.<\/p>\n<p>For every real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> and every positive normal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\)<\/span>, direct substitution gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widehat p(e^{-s}\\phi)=\\theta_s(\\widehat p(\\phi)).\n \\tag{OR13}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widehat p(e^{-s}\\phi)=\\theta_s(\\widehat p(\\phi)).\n \\tag{OR13}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\)<\/span>, its normalized state is unchanged by this scaling, while its new mass is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-s}m\\)\">\\(e^{-s}m\\)<\/span>; the group parameter in (OR12) becomes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s-\\log m\\)\">\\(s-\\log m\\)<\/span>. For zero both sides are zero. This is the scalar-equivariant projection statement with a domain on which both sides are defined.<\/p>\n<p>Only the state restriction was asserted to separate orbits by a projection alone. On the whole positive cone the pair\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\phi(1),\\widehat p(\\phi))\\)\">\\((\\phi(1),\\widehat p(\\phi))\\)<\/span> separates closed orbits: equal positive masses let us undo the same <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_{-\\log m}\\)\">\\(\\theta_{-\\log m}\\)<\/span> and apply the state result; mass zero forces both functionals to be zero. Conversely equal closed positive orbits have equal masses and equal normalized closed state orbits. In the scalar-center case, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat p\\)\">\\(\\widehat p\\)<\/span> is simply one on every nonzero functional, illustrating why that additional mass coordinate cannot be discarded.<\/p>\n<p>A normal conjugacy of center flows transports these projections to any other normal flow chart. It preserves their equality and generated von Neumann algebra. It also preserves the bounded strong Borel structure: the defining normal-positive-functional seminorms pull back along the isomorphism and its normal inverse. Intertwining the two actions transports (OR13) with the same parameter and sign.<\/p>\n<p>The human source is U. Haagerup and E. Størmer, <a href=\"https://www.isibang.ac.in/~soumyashant/misc/collected-works-of-haagerup/1990s/1990_Equivalence_of_normal_states_on_von_Neumann_algebras_and_the_flow_of_weights.pdf\"><em>Equivalence of Normal States on von Neumann Algebras and the Flow of Weights<\/em><\/a>, Advances in Mathematics 83 (1990), 180–262, especially §§3–8. This theorem retains the separable-predual infinite-factor scope; it does not assert the paper's further arbitrary-cardinality extension.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-CRNG.md","start_offset":9933,"end_offset":25513,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"634a95176ab6b421da4532dd543ffc3136dafabd7dc1f58f1030b55b9d9852b4":{"markdown":"### 9. Dominance forces infinite fixed multiplicity for an infinite group\n<a id=\"tic-9\"><\/a><a id=\"oa-flow.tic.9\"><\/a>\n\nFor a nonzero M, the action \\(\\alpha\\) is **dominant** when there is a unital normal isomorphism, with normal inverse,\n\\[\n \\theta:(M,\\alpha)\\longrightarrow\n (M\\overline\\otimes B(K),\\alpha\\overline\\otimes\\operatorname{Ad}\\rho)\n \\tag{TIC27}\n\\]\nintertwining every group parameter. This definition alone contains no proper-infiniteness clause.\n\n**Theorem 9.** If G is infinite and \\(\\alpha\\) is dominant, then \\(M^\\alpha\\) is properly infinite. This assertion needs no separability hypothesis. If G is abelian, dominance also supplies a unitary eigenvector in M for every character of G, whether G is finite or infinite.\n\n**Proof.** On \\(K\\otimes K\\) define\n\\[\n (T\\xi)(r,t)=\\xi(r,tr),\\qquad\n (T^{-1}\\eta)(r,q)=\\eta(r,qr^{-1}).\n \\tag{TIC28}\n\\]\nThe changes of variables are inverse homeomorphisms. On compactly supported continuous fields, right invariance in the second variable and qualified product integration prove the norm identity. Their compact carriers permit the earlier product theorem, and density extends T and its inverse to all of \\(K\\otimes K\\). Direct evaluation gives\n\\[\n T(\\rho_s\\otimes\\rho_s)T^*=\\rho_s\\otimes1.\n \\tag{TIC29}\n\\]\nFor example, both \\(T(\\rho_s\\otimes\\rho_s)\\xi\\) and \\((\\rho_s\\otimes1)T\\xi\\), at (r,t), are \\(\\xi(rs,trs)\\). Thus (TIC29) holds without commutativity.\n\nND extends \\(\\theta\\) and its inverse normally after tensoring with the third scalar factor. Consequently\n\\[\n \\Phi=(\\theta^{-1}\\overline\\otimes\\mathrm{id})\n        (\\mathrm{id}\\overline\\otimes\\operatorname{Ad}T)\n        (\\theta\\overline\\otimes\\mathrm{id})\\theta\n \\tag{TIC30}\n\\]\nis a whole normal isomorphism from (M,\\(\\alpha\\)) onto (\\(M\\overline\\otimes B(K)\\),\\(\\alpha\\overline\\otimes\\mathrm{id}\\)). Its whole inverse is the composition of the displayed inverses in reverse order. Formula (TIC29) checks its equivariance. Fixed matrix entries, with arbitrary-basis finite-coordinate compression, give\n\\[\n M^\\alpha\\cong M^\\alpha\\overline\\otimes B(K).\n \\tag{TIC31}\n\\]\nIf G is infinite, for every positive integer n choose n distinct points and disjoint relatively compact open neighborhoods. Each has positive finite right-Haar measure, so its normalized indicator gives one of n orthogonal vectors in K. Hence K has infinite dimension. An infinite-dimensional Hilbert space contains two orthogonal isometric copies of itself, by partitioning an orthonormal basis into two subsets of the same cardinality. Their isometries in B(K) make the right side of (TIC31) properly infinite; pull them back through Phi.\n\nFor abelian G and a character \\(\\chi\\), multiplication by \\(\\chi\\) is unitary on K and\n\\[\n \\rho_sM_\\chi\\rho_s^*=\\chi(s)M_\\chi.\n \\tag{TIC32}\n\\]\nThis is the identity \\(\\chi(ts)=\\chi(t)\\chi(s)\\), with the right-translation sign already fixed. Pull back \\(1\\otimes M_\\chi\\) through \\(\\theta\\) to obtain the claimed eigenunitary. \\(\\square\\)","html":"<h3>9. Dominance forces infinite fixed multiplicity for an infinite group<\/h3>\n<p>For a nonzero M, the action <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span> is <strong>dominant<\/strong> when there is a unital normal isomorphism, with normal inverse,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\theta:(M,\\alpha)\\longrightarrow\n (M\\overline\\otimes B(K),\\alpha\\overline\\otimes\\operatorname{Ad}\\rho)\n \\tag{TIC27}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\theta:(M,\\alpha)\\longrightarrow\n (M\\overline\\otimes B(K),\\alpha\\overline\\otimes\\operatorname{Ad}\\rho)\n \\tag{TIC27}\n\\]<\/span>\nintertwining every group parameter. This definition alone contains no proper-infiniteness clause.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem 9.<\/strong> If G is infinite and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span> is dominant, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M^\\alpha\\)\">\\(M^\\alpha\\)<\/span> is properly infinite. This assertion needs no separability hypothesis. If G is abelian, dominance also supplies a unitary eigenvector in M for every character of G, whether G is finite or infinite.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\otimes K\\)\">\\(K\\otimes K\\)<\/span> define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (T\\xi)(r,t)=\\xi(r,tr),\\qquad\n (T^{-1}\\eta)(r,q)=\\eta(r,qr^{-1}).\n \\tag{TIC28}\n\\]\">\\[\n (T\\xi)(r,t)=\\xi(r,tr),\\qquad\n (T^{-1}\\eta)(r,q)=\\eta(r,qr^{-1}).\n \\tag{TIC28}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe changes of variables are inverse homeomorphisms. On compactly supported continuous fields, right invariance in the second variable and qualified product integration prove the norm identity. Their compact carriers permit the earlier product theorem, and density extends T and its inverse to all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\otimes K\\)\">\\(K\\otimes K\\)<\/span>. Direct evaluation gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T(\\rho_s\\otimes\\rho_s)T^*=\\rho_s\\otimes1.\n \\tag{TIC29}\n\\]\">\\[\n T(\\rho_s\\otimes\\rho_s)T^*=\\rho_s\\otimes1.\n \\tag{TIC29}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor example, both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T(\\rho_s\\otimes\\rho_s)\\xi\\)\">\\(T(\\rho_s\\otimes\\rho_s)\\xi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\rho_s\\otimes1)T\\xi\\)\">\\((\\rho_s\\otimes1)T\\xi\\)<\/span>, at (r,t), are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi(rs,trs)\\)\">\\(\\xi(rs,trs)\\)<\/span>. Thus (TIC29) holds without commutativity.<\/p>\n<p>ND extends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> and its inverse normally after tensoring with the third scalar factor. Consequently\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\Phi=(\\theta^{-1}\\overline\\otimes\\mathrm{id})\n        (\\mathrm{id}\\overline\\otimes\\operatorname{Ad}T)\n        (\\theta\\overline\\otimes\\mathrm{id})\\theta\n \\tag{TIC30}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\Phi=(\\theta^{-1}\\overline\\otimes\\mathrm{id})\n        (\\mathrm{id}\\overline\\otimes\\operatorname{Ad}T)\n        (\\theta\\overline\\otimes\\mathrm{id})\\theta\n \\tag{TIC30}\n\\]<\/span>\nis a whole normal isomorphism from (M,<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span>) onto (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\overline\\otimes B(K)\\)\">\\(M\\overline\\otimes B(K)\\)<\/span>,<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\overline\\otimes\\mathrm{id}\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\overline\\otimes\\mathrm{id}\\)<\/span>). Its whole inverse is the composition of the displayed inverses in reverse order. Formula (TIC29) checks its equivariance. Fixed matrix entries, with arbitrary-basis finite-coordinate compression, give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n M^\\alpha\\cong M^\\alpha\\overline\\otimes B(K).\n \\tag{TIC31}\n\\]\">\\[\n M^\\alpha\\cong M^\\alpha\\overline\\otimes B(K).\n \\tag{TIC31}\n\\]<\/span>\nIf G is infinite, for every positive integer n choose n distinct points and disjoint relatively compact open neighborhoods. Each has positive finite right-Haar measure, so its normalized indicator gives one of n orthogonal vectors in K. Hence K has infinite dimension. An infinite-dimensional Hilbert space contains two orthogonal isometric copies of itself, by partitioning an orthonormal basis into two subsets of the same cardinality. Their isometries in B(K) make the right side of (TIC31) properly infinite; pull them back through Phi.<\/p>\n<p>For abelian G and a character <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi\\)\">\\(\\chi\\)<\/span>, multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi\\)\">\\(\\chi\\)<\/span> is unitary on K and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\rho_sM_\\chi\\rho_s^*=\\chi(s)M_\\chi.\n \\tag{TIC32}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\rho_sM_\\chi\\rho_s^*=\\chi(s)M_\\chi.\n \\tag{TIC32}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is the identity <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi(ts)=\\chi(t)\\chi(s)\\)\">\\(\\chi(ts)=\\chi(t)\\chi(s)\\)<\/span>, with the right-translation sign already fixed. Pull back <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\otimes M_\\chi\\)\">\\(1\\otimes M_\\chi\\)<\/span> through <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta\\)\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> to obtain the claimed eigenunitary. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-TIC.md","start_offset":28868,"end_offset":31827,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"6371754af542f80ea8fcc3c342cfed09259fbcca0c9d3a50e2a1399a6187d7c5":{"markdown":"#### The separation theorem\n\nTwo subsets \\(E,E'\\) of a Borel space are *Borel-separated* if some Borel set \\(B\\) has \\(E\\subseteq B\\) and \\(B\\cap E'=\\varnothing\\).\n\n**Lemma 2.3.** Let \\(E_n,E'_m\\) (\\(n,m\\in\\mathbb N\\)) be subsets of a Borel space, and for all \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) let \\(B_{n,m}\\) be a Borel set that contains \\(E_n\\) and misses \\(E'_m\\). Then \\(\\bigcup_nE_n\\) and \\(\\bigcup_mE'_m\\) are Borel-separated by\n\\[\nB=\\bigcup_n\\bigcap_mB_{n,m}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n**Proof.** \\(E_n\\subseteq\\bigcap_mB_{n,m}\\subseteq B\\). If \\(x\\in B\\), then \\(x\\in B_{n,m}\\) for one \\(n\\) and every \\(m\\), so \\(x\\) lies in no \\(E'_m\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\nSo, by (2), if \\(\\bigcup_nE_n\\) and \\(\\bigcup_mE'_m\\) are not Borel-separated, then some pair \\(E_n,E'_m\\) is not Borel-separated.\n\n**Theorem 2.4** (Lusin's separation theorem). Let \\(X\\) be a Hausdorff space and \\((X_n)_{n\\in I}\\), with \\(I\\) countable, pairwise disjoint Polish images in \\(X\\). There are pairwise disjoint Borel sets \\(B_n\\) with \\(X_n\\subseteq B_n\\). In particular this holds for pairwise disjoint Souslin sets of a metrizable space.\n\n\n**Proof.** *Two sets.* Let \\(Y,Y'\\) be disjoint Polish images. If \\(Y=\\varnothing\\), take \\(B=\\varnothing\\); if \\(Y'=\\varnothing\\), take \\(B=X\\). Otherwise there are continuous maps \\(f,f':\\Lambda\\to X\\) with \\(f(\\Lambda)=Y\\) and \\(f'(\\Lambda)=Y'\\): compose continuous maps of \\(\\Lambda\\) onto the Polish spaces ([Theorem 1.1](#oa-fnd-pb-01)) with the given maps. Put \\(Y_s=f(\\Lambda_s)\\) and \\(Y'_s=f'(\\Lambda_s)\\); then \\(Y_s=\\bigcup_nY_{sn}\\) and \\(Y'_s=\\bigcup_nY'_{sn}\\).\n\nSuppose \\(Y\\) and \\(Y'\\) are not Borel-separated. By Lemma 2.3 there are \\(n_1,m_1\\) with \\(Y_{(n_1)}\\) and \\(Y'_{(m_1)}\\) not Borel-separated. Applying the lemma again, to \\(Y_{(n_1)}=\\bigcup_nY_{(n_1,n)}\\) and \\(Y'_{(m_1)}=\\bigcup_mY'_{(m_1,m)}\\), and so on, we get \\(t,t'\\in\\Lambda\\) such that \\(Y_{t|k}\\) and \\(Y'_{t'|k}\\) are not Borel-separated for any \\(k\\). The points \\(f(t)\\in Y\\) and \\(f'(t')\\in Y'\\) are different, because \\(Y\\) and \\(Y'\\) are disjoint. Since \\(X\\) is Hausdorff, they have disjoint open neighbourhoods \\(V\\) and \\(W\\). The open set \\(f^{-1}(V)\\) contains \\(t\\), hence contains \\(\\Lambda_{t|k}\\) for all large \\(k\\); likewise \\(f'^{-1}(W)\\supseteq\\Lambda_{t'|k}\\) for all large \\(k\\). For such \\(k\\), \\(Y_{t|k}\\subseteq V\\) and \\(Y'_{t'|k}\\subseteq W\\subseteq X\\setminus V\\). So the open set \\(V\\) separates them, which is a contradiction.\n\n*Countably many sets.* Number the index set \\(1,2,\\ldots\\). For \\(n\\neq m\\) choose a Borel set \\(B_{n,m}\\supseteq X_n\\) with \\(B_{n,m}\\cap X_m=\\varnothing\\). Put \\(C_n=\\bigcap_{m\\neq n}B_{n,m}\\) and \\(B_n=C_n\\setminus(C_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup C_{n-1})\\). These sets are Borel and pairwise disjoint. \\(X_n\\subseteq C_n\\), and for \\(j<n\\), \\(X_n\\cap C_j\\subseteq X_n\\cap B_{j,n}=\\varnothing\\). So \\(X_n\\subseteq B_n\\). \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Corollary 2.5** (Souslin's theorem). Let \\(X\\) be a Hausdorff space and \\(A\\subseteq X\\). If \\(A\\) and \\(X\\setminus A\\) are both Polish images, then \\(A\\) is Borel. In particular, in a metrizable space a Souslin set whose complement is a Souslin set is Borel.\n\n\n**Proof.** By Theorem 2.4, some Borel set contains \\(A\\) and misses \\(X\\setminus A\\). Such a set is \\(A\\). \\(\\square\\)","html":"<h4>The separation theorem<\/h4>\n<p>Two subsets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E,E&#x27;\\)\">\\(E,E&#x27;\\)<\/span> of a Borel space are <em>Borel-separated<\/em> if some Borel set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\subseteq B\\)\">\\(E\\subseteq B\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\cap E&#x27;=\\varnothing\\)\">\\(B\\cap E&#x27;=\\varnothing\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Lemma 2.3.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_n,E&#x27;_m\\)\">\\(E_n,E&#x27;_m\\)<\/span> (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n,m\\in\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(n,m\\in\\mathbb N\\)<\/span>) be subsets of a Borel space, and for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span> let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_{n,m}\\)\">\\(B_{n,m}\\)<\/span> be a Borel set that contains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_n\\)\">\\(E_n\\)<\/span> and misses <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E&#x27;_m\\)\">\\(E&#x27;_m\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bigcup_nE_n\\)\">\\(\\bigcup_nE_n\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bigcup_mE&#x27;_m\\)\">\\(\\bigcup_mE&#x27;_m\\)<\/span> are Borel-separated by\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nB=\\bigcup_n\\bigcap_mB_{n,m}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\">\\[\nB=\\bigcup_n\\bigcap_mB_{n,m}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_n\\subseteq\\bigcap_mB_{n,m}\\subseteq B\\)\">\\(E_n\\subseteq\\bigcap_mB_{n,m}\\subseteq B\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in B\\)\">\\(x\\in B\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in B_{n,m}\\)\">\\(x\\in B_{n,m}\\)<\/span> for one <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> and every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> lies in no <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E&#x27;_m\\)\">\\(E&#x27;_m\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p>So, by (2), if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bigcup_nE_n\\)\">\\(\\bigcup_nE_n\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bigcup_mE&#x27;_m\\)\">\\(\\bigcup_mE&#x27;_m\\)<\/span> are not Borel-separated, then some pair <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_n,E&#x27;_m\\)\">\\(E_n,E&#x27;_m\\)<\/span> is not Borel-separated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Theorem 2.4<\/strong> (Lusin's separation theorem). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> be a Hausdorff space and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((X_n)_{n\\in I}\\)\">\\((X_n)_{n\\in I}\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> countable, pairwise disjoint Polish images in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>. There are pairwise disjoint Borel sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_n\\)\">\\(B_n\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_n\\subseteq B_n\\)\">\\(X_n\\subseteq B_n\\)<\/span>. In particular this holds for pairwise disjoint Souslin sets of a metrizable space.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> <em>Two sets.<\/em> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y,Y&#x27;\\)\">\\(Y,Y&#x27;\\)<\/span> be disjoint Polish images. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y=\\varnothing\\)\">\\(Y=\\varnothing\\)<\/span>, take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=\\varnothing\\)\">\\(B=\\varnothing\\)<\/span>; if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y&#x27;=\\varnothing\\)\">\\(Y&#x27;=\\varnothing\\)<\/span>, take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=X\\)\">\\(B=X\\)<\/span>. Otherwise there are continuous maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f,f&#x27;:\\Lambda\\to X\\)\">\\(f,f&#x27;:\\Lambda\\to X\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(\\Lambda)=Y\\)\">\\(f(\\Lambda)=Y\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f&#x27;(\\Lambda)=Y&#x27;\\)\">\\(f&#x27;(\\Lambda)=Y&#x27;\\)<\/span>: compose continuous maps of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda\\)\">\\(\\Lambda\\)<\/span> onto the Polish spaces (<a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-fnd-pb-01\">Theorem 1.1<\/a>) with the given maps. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_s=f(\\Lambda_s)\\)\">\\(Y_s=f(\\Lambda_s)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y&#x27;_s=f&#x27;(\\Lambda_s)\\)\">\\(Y&#x27;_s=f&#x27;(\\Lambda_s)\\)<\/span>; then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_s=\\bigcup_nY_{sn}\\)\">\\(Y_s=\\bigcup_nY_{sn}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y&#x27;_s=\\bigcup_nY&#x27;_{sn}\\)\">\\(Y&#x27;_s=\\bigcup_nY&#x27;_{sn}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\)\">\\(Y\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y&#x27;\\)\">\\(Y&#x27;\\)<\/span> are not Borel-separated. By Lemma 2.3 there are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n_1,m_1\\)\">\\(n_1,m_1\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_{(n_1)}\\)\">\\(Y_{(n_1)}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y&#x27;_{(m_1)}\\)\">\\(Y&#x27;_{(m_1)}\\)<\/span> not Borel-separated. Applying the lemma again, to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_{(n_1)}=\\bigcup_nY_{(n_1,n)}\\)\">\\(Y_{(n_1)}=\\bigcup_nY_{(n_1,n)}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y&#x27;_{(m_1)}=\\bigcup_mY&#x27;_{(m_1,m)}\\)\">\\(Y&#x27;_{(m_1)}=\\bigcup_mY&#x27;_{(m_1,m)}\\)<\/span>, and so on, we get <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t,t&#x27;\\in\\Lambda\\)\">\\(t,t&#x27;\\in\\Lambda\\)<\/span> such that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_{t|k}\\)\">\\(Y_{t|k}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y&#x27;_{t&#x27;|k}\\)\">\\(Y&#x27;_{t&#x27;|k}\\)<\/span> are not Borel-separated for any <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>. The points <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(t)\\in Y\\)\">\\(f(t)\\in Y\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f&#x27;(t&#x27;)\\in Y&#x27;\\)\">\\(f&#x27;(t&#x27;)\\in Y&#x27;\\)<\/span> are different, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\)\">\\(Y\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y&#x27;\\)\">\\(Y&#x27;\\)<\/span> are disjoint. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> is Hausdorff, they have disjoint open neighbourhoods <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span>. The open set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f^{-1}(V)\\)\">\\(f^{-1}(V)\\)<\/span> contains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>, hence contains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda_{t|k}\\)\">\\(\\Lambda_{t|k}\\)<\/span> for all large <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>; likewise <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f&#x27;^{-1}(W)\\supseteq\\Lambda_{t&#x27;|k}\\)\">\\(f&#x27;^{-1}(W)\\supseteq\\Lambda_{t&#x27;|k}\\)<\/span> for all large <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>. For such <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_{t|k}\\subseteq V\\)\">\\(Y_{t|k}\\subseteq V\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y&#x27;_{t&#x27;|k}\\subseteq W\\subseteq X\\setminus V\\)\">\\(Y&#x27;_{t&#x27;|k}\\subseteq W\\subseteq X\\setminus V\\)<\/span>. So the open set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span> separates them, which is a contradiction.<\/p>\n<p><em>Countably many sets.<\/em> Number the index set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1,2,\\ldots\\)\">\\(1,2,\\ldots\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\neq m\\)\">\\(n\\neq m\\)<\/span> choose a Borel set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_{n,m}\\supseteq X_n\\)\">\\(B_{n,m}\\supseteq X_n\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_{n,m}\\cap X_m=\\varnothing\\)\">\\(B_{n,m}\\cap X_m=\\varnothing\\)<\/span>. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_n=\\bigcap_{m\\neq n}B_{n,m}\\)\">\\(C_n=\\bigcap_{m\\neq n}B_{n,m}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_n=C_n\\setminus(C_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup C_{n-1})\\)\">\\(B_n=C_n\\setminus(C_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup C_{n-1})\\)<\/span>. These sets are Borel and pairwise disjoint. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_n\\subseteq C_n\\)\">\\(X_n\\subseteq C_n\\)<\/span>, and for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j&lt;n\\)\">\\(j&lt;n\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_n\\cap C_j\\subseteq X_n\\cap B_{j,n}=\\varnothing\\)\">\\(X_n\\cap C_j\\subseteq X_n\\cap B_{j,n}=\\varnothing\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_n\\subseteq B_n\\)\">\\(X_n\\subseteq B_n\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Corollary 2.5<\/strong> (Souslin's theorem). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> be a Hausdorff space and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\subseteq X\\)\">\\(A\\subseteq X\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\setminus A\\)\">\\(X\\setminus A\\)<\/span> are both Polish images, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is Borel. In particular, in a metrizable space a Souslin set whose complement is a Souslin set is Borel.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> By Theorem 2.4, some Borel set contains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> and misses <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\setminus A\\)\">\\(X\\setminus A\\)<\/span>. Such a set is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/polish-spaces-and-standard-borel-spaces.md","start_offset":19317,"end_offset":22553,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"}});
